2300:
** θεάομαι**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **θηέομαι** (see. below), _Doric dialect_ **θᾱέομαι**, **Θάομαι** (which see), _imperative_ θεῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 262}}; _optative_ θηοῖο (for _Attic dialect_ θεῷο) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.418}}; _participle_ θηεύμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.146: _Ionic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἐθηεῖτο, ἐθηεῦντο, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.10, 3.136}}; _Epic dialect_ θηεῖτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.75, etc., θηεῦντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.444, +others, ἐθηεύμεσθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.218, ἐθεῆτο 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 13, θηέσκετο}} poetical cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 21.2:}}_future_ θεάσομαι [ᾱ], _Ionic dialect_ -ήσομαι: _aorist_ ἐθεᾱσάμην, _Epic dialect_ _optative_ θηήσαιο, θηήσαιτο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.315, 5.74}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐθηήσαντο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 51.15}}; _Ionic dialect_ _infinitive_ θεήσασθαι (variant{θεάσ-}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}: _Attic dialect_ _perfect_ τεθέαμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.7: codices of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus vary between θεη- and θηη-: a rare _Ionic dialect_ _contraction_ of θηη- to θη-}} is found in θησαίατ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.191, θησάμενος “IG” 12.826}}:—**gaze at, behold**, mostly with a sense of **wonder**, θηεῦντο μέγα ἔργον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.444, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.13; λαοὶ δ᾽ αὖ θηεῦντό τε θάμβησάν τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.728, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 11, etc.; θ. τὰ καλά 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 194; πάντες ὥσπερ ἄγαλμα ἐθεῶντο αὐτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 154c; θ. ὄμμασι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 232 (Lyric poetry); ζητεῖ τὸ κακὸν τεθεᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 797 codices}}; ἐθεᾶτο.. τὴν θέσιν τῆς πόλεως.., ὡς ἔχοι **reconnoitred** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.7; θ. κύκλῳ τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.7: _absolutely_, θεᾷ}}; **do you see?** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 564.}}
**2** of the mind, **contemplate**, τὸ ἀληθές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84b, +others}}
**2.b** **see clearly**, ἵν᾽ ἴδητε καὶ θεάσησθε ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 352a; with _relative_ clause, ὅση δεινότης ἦν ἐν τῷ Φιλίππῳ θεάσεσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.144.}}
**3** **view as spectators**, especially in the theatre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.44}}; οἱ θεώμενοι **the spectators**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 2, compare “Nu.” 518, +others}} (but also, **onlookers, bystanders**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7): _metaphorically_, θ. τὸν πόλεμον}} **to be spectators of** the war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.116.}}
**4** θ. τὸ στράτευμα **to review** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.1.}}
**II** _active_ **θεάω**, late, Baillet **Tombeaux des rois à Thèbes** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|1080: elsewhere in _imperative_ θέα 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 3.44b, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 89b, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus:}}_aorist_ ἐθεάθην in _passive_ sense, Ps. -Callisth.{{NT+1st c.AD+|2.42, NT.Mark.16.11, 1st c.AD: Apollonius Tyanensis Epistolographus “Epistulae” 49, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 133.3.1}}: _present_ θεῶνται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.9. (Orig. probably θᾱϝ έομαι and θᾱϝάομαι, compare θαῦ-μα.)}}

2301:
** θεᾱτρ-ίζω**,
**to be** or **play on the stage**, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**II** _passive_, **to be made a show of, held up to shame,** {{NT|NT.Heb.10.33.}}

2302:
** θέᾱτρον**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θέητρον**, τό, (θεάομαι) **place for seeing**, especially**for dramatic representation, theatre**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.67, “IG” 22.1176, +others}}; as **a place of assembly**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.32, “SIG” 976.4 (Samos, 2nd c.BC), 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.Act.19.20, etc.}}; θ. κυνηγετικόν, of the Roman **amphitheatre**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.22}}; εἰς τὸ θ. εἰσφέρειν to bring upon **the stage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.122}}; τὸ καλὸν τοῦ θ. a good place in the **theatre**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.13, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.20.}}
**2** collective for οἱ θεαταί, **the spectators, 'the house**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 233, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194b, “Comica Adespota” 3D.: _metaphorically_, ἐκάθηντο θέατρον αὐτῷ 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 722.4.}}
**3** ={θέαμα}, **spectacle**, θ. ἐγενήθημεν τῷ κόσμῳ {{NT|NT.1Cor.4.9.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, of **life**, τουτὶ τὸ θ. ὑπεκρίθημεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.}}

2304:
** θεῖος**, α, ον:
**A**late _Epic dialect_ **θέειος** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hymni” 2.16}}; **θεήϊος** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 15.9}}; late _Aeolic dialect_ **θήϊος** {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 989.4 (from Balbilla)}}; _Laconian dialect_ **σεῖος** (see. below 1.3): _comparative_ and _superlative_ θειότερος, -ότατος, frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Phdr.” 279a, “Mx.” 244d, +others}}: (θεός):
**1** **of** or **from the gods, divine**, γένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.180; ὀμφή 2.41}}; Ὄνειρος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 22; ἐπιπνοίαις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 577, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499c; μάστιξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 682; μανία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 611 (Lyric poetry); νόσος [prev. work] 185 (Lyric poetry) (but θ. νόσος}}, of a dust-storm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 421); κίνδυνοι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139}}; θ. τινὶ μοίρᾳ by **divine** intervention, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.10; θ. τύχῃ γεγονώς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126; θ. τύχῃ χρεώμενος [prev. author] 3.139}}; θ. κἀπόνῳ τύχῃ, of an easy death, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1585; ἐκ θ. τύχης [prev. author] “Ph.” 1326; ἔμαθε ὡς θ. εἴη τὸ πρῆγμα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69; ὁ θ. νόμος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; φύσις θ. {{NT|“SIG” 1125.8 (from Eleusis), cf. NT.2Pet.1.4}}; **appointed of God**, βασιλῆες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.691}}; σκῆπτρον **given by God**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 139 (Lyric poetry); see below 2.}}
**2** **belonging** or **sacred to a god, holy**, ἀγών, χορός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.298, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.264}}; **under divine protection**, πύργος, δόμος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.526, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.43}}; of heralds and bards, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.17, +others}}; so perhaps, of kings,{{Refs|[prev. work] 691.}}
**3** **morethan human**, of heroes, Ὀδυσσεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.335, +others, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 144.4 (Lyric poetry); θ. ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.38, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1548 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331e, “Men.” 99d (especially at Sparta (_Laconian dialect_ σεῖος), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1145a29}}; ὦ θεῖε (in the mouth of a Spartan) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 626c); μετὰ σοῦ τῆς θείας κεφαλῆς [prev. author] “Phdr.” 234d, compare 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 9.128a, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 19.66.}}
**3.b** of things, **excellent**, θεῖον ποτόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.341, 9.205; ἁλὸς θείοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.214}}; θ. πρήγματα **marvellous** things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.66; ἐν τοῖσι θειότατον [prev. author] 7.137.}}
**4** = Latin _divinus_ (or **sacer**), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Imperial”, διατάξεις probably in “BGU” 473.5 (200 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), etc.; θησαυροί “PLips.” 62ii14 (4th c.AD)}}; θ. ὅρκος oath **by the Emperor,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|POxy. 83.6 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; θειότατος, of living Emperors, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 105.22 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**4.b** = Latin _divus_, of deified Emperors, θ. Σεβαστός Edict.Claud. cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.5.3, compare “Inscription Perg.” 283 (3rd c.AD), 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 2.3.}}
**II** as _substantive_, θεῖον, τό, **the Divinity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 3.108, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 958 (Lyric poetry); τοῦ θ. χάριν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.70}}; ἡμαρτηκότα εἰς τὸ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242c.}}
**II.2** in an abstract sense, **divinity**, κεκοινώνηκε.. τοῦ θ. {{Refs|[prev. work] 246d}}; ἢ μόνον μετέχει τοῦ θ..., ἢ μάλιστα [ἄνθρωπος] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 656a8, etc.}}; κατὰ θεῖον or κατά τι θ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Aeneas Gazaeus Rhetor “Theophrastus” p.37 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, p.4 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II.3** θεῖα, τά, **the acts of the gods, course of providence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 452, etc.; τὰ θ. θνητοὺς ὄντας εὐπετῶς φέρειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 585; τὰ θ. μὴ φαύλως φέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 961.}}
**II.3.b** **matters of religion**, ἔρρει τὰ θ. **religion** is no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 910 (Lyric poetry), compare “OC” 1537, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2, etc.}}
**II.3.c** **inquiries concerning the divine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 232c}}; τὰ φανερὰ τῶν θείων, i.e. the heavenly bodies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1026a18, compare “GA” 731b24, “Ph.” 196a33 (_superlative_), “EN” 1141b1.}}
**III** _adverb_ θείως **by divine providence**, θ. πως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.1, etc.}}; θειοτέρως **by special providence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122; μᾶλλόν τι καὶ -ότερον [prev. work] 174.}}
**III.2** **divinely, excellently**, εὖ γε καὶ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154d; θείως εἰρῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b9.}}

2305:
** θειότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**divine nature, divinity**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.18.9, NT.Rom.1.20, “SIG” 867.31 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.665a, etc.}}
**2** _falsa lectio_ for{ὁσιότης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.26, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.857a, and so probably in [prev. author] “Sull.” 6.}}
**II** as title of Roman Emperors, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1.1.1, “SIG” 900.23 (Panamara, 4th c.AD), etc.}}

2306:
** θειώδης**, ες,
(θεῖον A)
**A** **sulphureous**, of waters, etc., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 24.45, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.2.3, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 3.167, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 7.5; ὀδμή 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.18.}}
**A.2** of colour, **yellow**, θώρακες {{NT|NT.Rev.9.17.}}

2307:
** θέλ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
(θέλω) **will**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 58 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 18.19, LXX.Est.1.8, +others, NT.Matt.7.21, “POxy.” 924.8 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** ἔστιν μοι θ. ἔν τινι **pleasure** in.., {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.12.1, compare 5.3}}:—also **θελ-ήμη**, ἡ, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 112.}}

2308:
** θέλ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**a willing, will,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|Stoic. 3.41, LXX.Tob.12.18, +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.297 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, NT.Heb.2.4; condemned by 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.165: in _Doric dialect_ _plural_ θελήσιες}}, **wishes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus “Ep.”}}
**II** **goodwill, favour**, {{LXX|LXX.Pro.8.35.}}

2310:
** θεμέλῐ-ος**, ον,
**of** or **for the foundation**, λίθοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1137; οἰκόπεδα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.66: _absolutely_, θεμέλιος (i.e. λίθος)}}, ὁ, **foundation-stone**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 237b13, “Metaphorical” 1013a5: _metaphorically_, τῆς τέχνης θ. 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.346a; θ. ἀγνοίας 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.266}}; οἱ θ. ἐκ παντοίων λίθων ὑπόκεινται the **foundations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93; τοὺς θ. ἐκ τῶν λίθων οἰκοδομεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668a19: _metaphorically_, προλιπεῖντοὺς προγονικοὺς θ. “SIG” 888.70(Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD)}}: also _neuter_ θεμέλιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 95b37 (assuming variant), “PPetr.” 3p.121 (3rd c.BC), +others: _plural_, τὰ θ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 200a4, “PCair.Zen.” 176.71 (3rd c.BC), +others, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.32.1: _metaphorically_, τὰ ὑποβληθέντα θ.}}, of the **foundations** of the world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.38U.: gender indeterminate, μὴ ὑποκειμένων.. θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.2}}; ἐκ τῶν θ. from the **foundations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68 (also _sing._, ἐνέπρησαν [οἰκίαν] ἐκ θεμελίου “BGU” 909.17 (4th c.AD))}}: _metaphorically_, ἐκθ. ἐσφαλμένοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.93.2, etc.; ἄρδην καὶ ἐκ θ. ἀπόλλυσθαι 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.3.2}}; also ἀνεκτίσθη τὸ τεῖχος ἐκ θεμελείων (sic) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 2.480(Kuban, 4th c.AD).}}
**II** θεμέλια, τά, **buildingsites**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 174, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 82.24, +others}}
**III** _substantive_, **the fourth** τόπος,= ἀντιμεσουράνημα, Herm.Trism. in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).101, compare 8(4).241.}}

2311:
** θεμελῐ-όω**,
**to lay the foundation of, found firmly**, πύργους.. φοίνιξι θεμελιώσας {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.11, compare “IG” 12(2).11.26 (Mytil.), LXX.Josh.6.25 (26), NT.Heb.1.10, etc.}}:—_passive_, **have the foundations laid,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1343.15 (1st c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ τὴν πέτραν {{NT+1st c.BC+|NT.Matt.7.25: _metaphorically_, βασιλεία καλῶς θεμελιωθεῖσα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.68; ἡγεμονία κάλλιστα τεθεμελιωμένη [prev. author] 15.1}}; ἐν ἀγάπῃ τεθ. {{NT|NT.Eph.3.18; τῇ πίστει NT.Col.1.23.}}
**II** **destroy utterly**, in _passive_, -ωθέντα (θεμειλωθ- codex)· ἐκ ῥιζῶν ἀρθέντα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2316:
** θεός**, ὁ,
_Boeotian dialect_ **θιός**, _Laconian dialect_ **σιός** (see. below), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria}}, Cretan dialect **θιός** {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.27 H., “Leg.Gort.” 1.1}}, _Doric dialect_ also **θεύς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 58; _accusative_ θεῦν variant [prev. work] 130}}; _vocative_ (only late) θεός, also θεέ {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.3.24, NT.Matt.27.46, “PMag.Lond.” 121.529, etc.}}; but classical in _compound_ names, Ἀμφίθεε, Τιμόθεε:—**God, the Deity**, in general sense, both _singular_ and _plural_ (εἰ καὶ ἐπὶ θεοὺς καὶ ἔτι μᾶλλον ἐπὶ θεὸν ἁρμόζει μεταφέρειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.8.1), θ. δὲ τὸ μὲν δώσει τὸ δ᾽ ἐάσει “God” will grant.., 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.444; οὐδέ κεν ἄλλως οὐδὲ θ. τεύξειε 8.177, compare 3.231, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.730 (also θεὸς Ζεύς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.236, 14.327); θ. καὶ ἀγαθὴ τύχη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757e, compare 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; σὺν θεῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.49, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 765, etc. (less frequently ξὺν τῷ θ. [prev. work] 383)}}; σὺν θ. εἰρημένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, compare 3.153; σὺν θ. εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317b}}: so in _plural_, σύν γε θεοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.430; οὔ τοι ἄνευ θεοῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.372}}; οὐ θεῶν ἄτερ pi.{{Refs 8th c.BC+|“P.” 5.76; ἐκ θεόφι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.101}}; ὑπὲρ θεόν **against his will**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|17.327; ἂν θ. θέλῃ 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 231; θ. θέλοντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 671: in _plural_, ἂν θεοὶ θέλωσιν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 247}}; θεῶν συνεθελόντων, βουλομένων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 9.8, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 29; εἰ ὀρθῶς ἢ μή, θ. οἶδε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266b, compare “R.” 517b, etc.; in oaths, θ. ἴστω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 522 (Lyric poetry), etc.; πρὸς θεῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15, etc.: τοὺς θεούς σοι}}**bless you! good heavens! for heaven's sake!** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.17, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.15, +others; τὸν θ. σοι [prev. work] 3.7.19, +others: qualified by τις, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.142, etc.; οὐκ ἄνευ θεῶν τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 164 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 764; κατὰ θεόν τινα [prev. author] “IA” 411, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272e; κατὰ θεόν πως εἰρημένα [prev. author] “Lg.” 682a}}: doubled in poets, θεὸν θεόν τις ἀγλαϊζέτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.21, compare 5th c.BC: Diagoras Lyricus 1; θεοὶ θεοὶ τῶν ἀδίκων μέλουσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 772, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Grammaticus “fragment” 203}}; θεοί (Cretan dialect θιοί) as an opening formula in Inscrr. (i.e. τύχην ἀγαθὴν διδοῖεν), {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.1, “IG” 12.52, etc.: _sing._, θ. τύχη [prev. work]5(2)}}.1, etc.: in Prose also with the _Article_, ὁ θ. πάντων ἂν εἴη αἴτιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379c, compare “Lg.” 716c, etc.}}; τὰ πρὸς τοὺς θ., τὰ παρὰ τῶν θ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.1, 2.6.8.}}
**b** θεοί, opposed to ἄνδρες, πατὴρ ἀνδρῶν τε θεῶν τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.544; ὃν Ξάνθον καλέουσι θ., ἄνδρες δὲ Σκάμανδρον 20.74: in Comparisons, θεοῖσιν ἶσ᾽ ἔθελε φρονέειν 5.440; θεοῖς ἐναλίγκια μήδεα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.89}}; also in _singular_, θεῷ ἐναλίγκιος αὐδήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.250; θεὸς ὥς 5.78; ὥς τε θεός 3.381: proverbial, θεὸς πρὸς ἀνθρώπους}}, of an 'angel's visit', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.9.}}
**c** of special divinities, νέρτεροι θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 622, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 602 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐνέρτεροι θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.225}}; οἱ κάτωθεν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1070; θ. οὐράνιοι “hymnus ad Cererem” 55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 90 (_anapaest meter_)}}; οἱ δώδεκα θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 235, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 3.2, “IG” 22.30, etc.}}; μὰ τοὺς δώδεκα θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 91}}; in dual, τὼ σιώ (_Laconian dialect_), of Castor and Pollux, ναὶ τὼ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.34, “HG” 4.4.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 81: so in _Boeotian dialect_, of Amphion and Zethus, νεὶ τὼ σιώ (to be read θιώ) [prev. author] “Ach.” 905.}}
**d** ὁ θ., of natural phenomena, ὁ θ. ὕει (i.e. Ζεύς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13; ὁ θ. ἐνέσκηψε βέλος [prev. author] 4.79}}; ἔσεισεν ὁ θ. (i.e. Ποσειδῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.7.4}}; of the sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.24, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 502, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 722}}; δύνοντος τοῦ θ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.79}}; the **weather**, τί δοκεῖ τὰ τοῦ θεο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 25.2.}}
**e** Astrology texts, θεοί,= ἀστέρες, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Laod.in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).252.}}
**f** θεός (i.e. Ἥλιος), name of the 9th τόπος, Rhetor.{{Refs|[prev. work]163, etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of abstract things, τὸ δ᾽ εὐτυχεῖν τόδ᾽ ἐν βροτοῖς θεός τε καὶ θεοῦ πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 60; ἡ φρόνησις ἁγαθὴ θ. μέγας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 922; θ. γὰρ καὶ τὸ γιγνώσκειν φίλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hel.” †60}}; ὁ πλοῦτος τοῖς σοφοῖς θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 316}}; φθόνος κάκιστος θ. {{Refs|Hippothoon 2.}}
**3** as title of rulers, θεῶν ἀδελφῶν (i.e. Ptolemy Il and Arsinoe), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.30, etc.; Πτολεμαῖος ὑπάρχων θεὸς ἐκ θεοῦ καὶ θεᾶς “OGI” 90.10 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; Ἀντίοχος ὅτῳ θεὸς ἐπώνυμον γίγνεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 65}}; θεὸς ἐκ θεοῦ, of Augustus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 655.2 (Egypt, 1st c.BC); θ. ἡμῶν καὶ δεσπότης “IPE” 4.71 (Cherson., 2nd c.AD).}}
**3.b** = Latin _Divus,_ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Mon.Anc.Gr. 10.4, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.1.1, etc.}}; οἱ ἐν θεοῖς αὐτοκράτορες,= **divi Imperatores,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(1).786 (from Rhodes).}}
**3.c** generally of the dead, καὶ ζῶντός σου καὶ εἰς θεοὺς ἀπελθόντος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.45 (3rd c.BC)}}; θεοῖς χθονίοις,= Latin _Dis Manibus,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.30, +others}}
**4** **one set in authority, judge**, τὸ κριτήριον τοῦ θ., ἐνώπιον τοῦ θ., {{LXX|LXX.Exo.21.6, 22.8; θεοὺς οὐ κακολογήσεις [prev. work] 22.28(27).}}
**II** θεός _feminine_, **goddess**, μήτε θήλεια θεός, μήτε τις ἄρσην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.7, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.35, +others; τοῖς θεοῖς εὔχομαι πᾶσι καὶ πάσαις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.1, compare 141, Oracle texts [prev. work] 21.52}}; especially at Athens, of Athena, Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21a, etc.}}; ἁ Διὸς θεός, Ζηνὸς ἡ θ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 401 (Lyric poetry), 952 (ἡ Διὸς θεά [prev. work] 450)}}; of other goddesses, ποντία θεός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).36}}; ἡ νερτέρα θ.,= Περσεφόνη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1548, etc.}}; of Thetis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c}}; of Niobe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 150 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 834 (_anapaest meter_)}}: in dual, of Demeter and Persephone, τὰ τοῖν θεοῖν ψηφίσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 378 (Lyric poetry); οὐδ᾽ ἔδεισε τὼ θεώ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.125; frequently in oaths, νὴ τὼ θεώ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 112; μὰ τὼ θεώ [prev. author] “Ec.” 155, 532.}}
**III** as _adjective_ in _comparative_ θεώτερος, **divine**, θύραι θ., opposed to καταιβαταὶ ἀνθρώποισιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.111}}; χορὸς θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 93, compare “Dian.” 249, 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 257. (Derived by 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.52 from τίθημι}} (κόσμῳ θέντες τὰ πρήγματα), by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 397d from θεῖν. Etym. uncertain)}} [In _Epic dialect_ (twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus) and Trag. (5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 47, 1347, +others, not in Comedy texts)}}, as _monosyllable_ by synizesis, θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.18, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 142; θεῶν “hymnus ad Cererem” 55, 259; θεοῖς 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 171; θεοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.251; θεούς “hymnus ad Cererem” 325: even in _nominative_ θεός before a vowel, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 399 (cf. Pors. (at [prev. work])), “HF” 347; in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.56 apparently a short monosyllable]}}

2322:
** θερᾰπ-εία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θερᾰπ-ηΐη** (θερᾰπ-είη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 80, +others)}}, ἡ, **service, attendance**:
**I** of persons, θ. τῶν θεῶν **service paid** to the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 13d, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 744 (Lyric poetry); θεῶν καὶ ἡρώων θεραπεῖαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427b, etc.; ἡ περὶ τοὺς θεοὺς θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.24}}; ἀγυιάτιδες θ. **worship** of Apollo Agyieus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 187; τὴν θ. ἀποδιδόναι τοῖς θεοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1329a32; θ. τῆς μήνιδος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.159b: _absolutely_, πᾶσαν θ. ὡς ἰσόθεος θεραπευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255a, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.4}}; of parents, γονέων θεραπείας καὶ τιμάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 886c, compare 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; of children, **nurture, care**, μικροὺς παῖδας θεραπείας δεομένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.45; θ. καὶ ἐσθής 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.4}}; θ. σώματος, ψυχῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464b, “La.” 185e.}}
**I.2** **service done to gain favour, paying court**, θ. τοῦ κοινοῦ καὶ τῶν αἰεὶ προεστώτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11; ἐν θεραπείᾳ ἔχειν πολλῇ [prev. author] 1.55; πάσῃ θεραπείᾳ θεραπεύειν τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.14; θεραπείαις προσαγαγέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.22; τῇ θ. ψυχαγωγούμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.55.}}
**II** **medical** or **surgical treatment** or **cure**, χειρός, ποδός, Hp.[same place]; αἱ ὑπὸ τῶν ἰατρῶν θ. αἱ διὰ καύσεων γιγνόμεναι **cures** by cautery, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 354a}}; ἡ ἐκ τῶν γραμμάτων θ. **treatment** secundum artem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a40, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.400, etc.; τῶν καμνόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 345a, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.21 W.; τοῦ σώματος [prev. author] “Lib.” p.19 O., “Vita Philonidis Epicurei” p.9 C.}}; **healing**, θεραπείας ἐπιτυχών {{Refs|“Sammelb.” 1537b}}: in _plural_, **cures**, ἰατρὸς ποιεῖ -είας {{Refs|“POxy.” 1r. 13.}}
**III** of animals, **care, tendance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 13a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 578a7 (_plural_).}}
**III.2** of plants, **cultivation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 149e, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.2.12.}}
**III.3** **maintenance** or **repairs** of temples, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1106.49 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC), 1102.8 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III.4** **preparation** of fat for medical use, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.76.}}
**IV** in collective sense, **body of attendants, retinue**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, 5.21, 7.184, LXX.Gen.45.16; σὺν ἱππικῇ θ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.1; ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς θ. τεταγμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.87.5.}}

2323:
** θερᾰπ-εύω**,
later also **θᾰρᾰπεύω** (which see), _future_ **-εύσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, etc.}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-εύσομαι**{{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 390}}: _aorist_ ἐθεραπευσάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.23.65 codices, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.295}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ευθήσομαι**{{Refs|[prev. author] 10.617}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.135e}}: _aorist_ ἐθεραπεύθην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 157b, etc.}}:—**to be an attendant, do service**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.265}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 390.}}
**II** **do service to** the gods, ἀθανάτους, θεοὺς θ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 135, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.13, etc.; δαίμονα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.109}}; Διόνυσον, Μούσας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 82 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 1105 (Lyric poetry)}}; θ. Φοίβου ναούς **serve** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 111 (_anapaest meter_)}}: _absolutely_, **worship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.51}}; **do service** or **honour to** one's parents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 183 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467a, “Men.” 91a}}; **serve, wait upon** a master, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthphr.” 13d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 59, 1261, etc.}}; θ. τὰς θήκας **reverence** men's graves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469a.}}
**II.2** in Prose, **pay court to**, [τινα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80, etc.}}; in bad sense, **flatter, wheedle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.12}}; θ. τὸ πλῆθος, τοὺς πολλούς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 1.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 34}}; **conciliate**, τινὰ χρημάτων δόσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.2.8}}; τὸ θεραπεῦον,= οἱ θεραπεύοντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; θ. γυναῖκα **pay** her **attention**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.18}}; also τὰς θύρας τινὸς θ. **wait at** a man's door,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.1.6; αὐλὰς θ. καὶ σατράπας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 897}}; αὐλὰς βασιλικὰς θ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.63.}}
**II.3** of things, **consult, attend to**, τὸ ξυμφέρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56}}; ἡδονὴν θ. **indulge** one's love of pleasure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.41}}; θ. τὸ παρόν **look to, provide for** the present, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 149 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; τὴν ἄνοιξιν τῶν πυλῶν [prev. author] 4.67; θ. τοὺς καιρούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.307}}: with _infinitive_, **take care that.. **, θ. τὸ μὴ θορυβεῖν, μὴ λείπεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61, 7.70; θ. ὅπως πολιτεύσουσι [prev. author] 1.19}}; θ. ὡς.. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 4.1.}}
**II.4** θ. τὸ σῶμα **take care of** one's person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 513d; θ. αὑτούς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 9; θ. τὰς τρίχας 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 4.4}}; μύροις χαίτην θ. {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 62.3; θ. τοὺς πόδας LXX.2Ki.19.24}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, θ. κόμην φαίνεσθαι λιπαράν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 22.}}
**II.5** **foster**, τὴν ψυχήν, τὴν διάνοιαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 440c, “R.” 403d}}; θ. κάδεα **brood over** sorrows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).8.}}
**II.6** θ. ἡμέρην **observe** a day, **keep** it **as a feast**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.79; ἱερὰ -όμενα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98.}}
**II.7** **treat medically**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47, 51; τοὺς τετρωμένους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12; τραύματα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 89; μὴ θεραπεύειν βέλτιον· θεραπευόμενοι γὰρ ἀπόλλυνται ταχέως 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 6.38; ταύτην τὴν θεραπείαν θεραπεύσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.4; θ. νόσημα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.28; σώματα -όμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684c; ὀφθαλμούς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102a19: _absolutely_, οἱ θεραπεύοντες 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.29 W.: _metaphorically_, ὁ κοινὸς ἰατρός σε θεραπεύσει χρόνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 32; λύπην.. οἶδε θεραπεύειν λόγος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 591; τὰ πονοῦντα μέρη τῆς νεώς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.41}}; τὰς ὑποψίας **allayed**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 22; ὑπόνοιαν 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus in “de An.” 408.3}}; δυστυχίαν **assuage** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 6.}}
**II.8** of animals, **train**, ἵππους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e.}}
**II.9** of land, **cultivate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.12}}; of trees, **train**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193; στέλεχος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.3.}}
**II.10** **prepare, dress**, food or drugs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 13.4, +others, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.76 (_passive_).}}
**II.11** **mend** garments, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 79iv3 (_passive_, 2nd c.AD).}}

2324:
** θερᾰπ-ων**, οντος, ὁ,
_dative_ _plural_ θεραπόντεσσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.41}}; _Aeolic dialect_ θεράπων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 74, _genitive_ θερράπονος Choerob.in “An Ox.” 2.242 (θεράπονος codex, cf. Hdn.Gr.2.302)}}:—**henchman, attendant**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.253, etc.}}; **companion in arms, squire**, {{Refs|4.23}}, etc.; ἡνίοχος θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.580, 8.119; τώ οἱ ἔσαν κήρυκε.. καὶ θεράποντε 1.321; θεράποντε Διός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.255; θεράποντες Ἄρηος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.110, etc.; Μουσάων θεράποντες “Hymni Homerici” 32.20, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 100, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 769, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 909 (Lyric poetry)}}; Ἔρως Ἀφροδίτης θ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203c, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [same place]}}; **worshipper**, Ἀπόλλωνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85a; Ἄρεος “BMus.Inscription” 971 (5th c.AD: Cypria, 5th c.BC): with _dative_, οἶκος ξένοισι θεράπων}} **devoted to the service of** its guests, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.3}}; λωτὸς.. Μουσᾶν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 717 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _genitive_, **attending upon**, τῶν ἀδίκως δυστυχούντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** **servant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5.105, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 3, 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, etc.}}; at Chios, **slave**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis ad 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 533.}}

2325:
** θερίζω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ _infinitive_ θερίδδειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 947 (Lyric poetry)}}: _future_ _Attic dialect_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 601b17}}: _aorist_ ἐθέρισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 239 (_anapaest meter_), syncopated op. ἔθρισα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 536 (compare ἀποθρίζω)}}; _poetry_ ἐθέρισσα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.451}}; later (_subjunctive_) ἐκθερίξω {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 9.7:}} —_middle_ (see. below):—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐθερίσθην: _perfect_ τεθέρισμαι (see. below): (θέρος):—**do summer-work, mow, reap**, σῖτον, κριθάς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 506, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **harvest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.71 K.: frequently _metaphorically_, joined with σπείρω, αἰσχρῶς μὲν ἔσπειρας κακῶς δὲ ἐθέρισας 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 16 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.394e; ἡ ῥητορικὴ καρπὸν ὧν ἔσπειρε θερίζει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260d; οὐκ ἔστι μὴ σπείραντα θερίσαι κάρπιμα “epigram Gr.” 1039.15}}:— _middle_, καρπὸν Δηοῦς θερίσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 515}}:—_passive_, ἃ [δράγματα] ἔτυχεν.. τεθερισμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.8.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **mow down**, Ἄρη τὸν.. θερίζοντα βροτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 637 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ag.” 536; βίον θ. ὥστε κάρπιμον στάχυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 34(60).94}}; θ. Ἀσίαν **to plunder** it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.182a.}}
**3** **cut off**, κεφαλὴν καὶ γλῶσσαν ἄκραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 239; κυνέας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 717; γλῶσσαν “Anthologia Graeca” 9.451: _metaphorically_, σελίδος νεαρῆς θ. στάχυν [prev. work]4.2.3 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus):}} —_passive_, ἥτις [πῶλος].. θέρος θερισθῇ ξανθὸν αὐχένων ἄπο who **had her crop** of yellow mane **cut off**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 659.4.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **reap a good harvest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 947 (Lyric poetry)}}; of bribes, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 47.26.}}
**5** ὁ θερίζων (with or without λόγος), a logical fallacy, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.94, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.25: _plural_, [prev. work] 44.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **pass the summer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.15; θ. ἐν τοῖς ψυχροῖς, χειμάζουσι δ᾽ ἐν τοῖς ἀλεεινοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 596b26, compare 598a25.}}

2326:
** θερ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**mowing, reaping**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.3, “PHib.” 1.90.5 (3rd c.BC), “PFlor.” 101.4 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **reaping-time, harvest**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 202, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.95.5, NT.Matt.13.30, +others}}
**II.2** **harvest, crop**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Lev.19.9, NT.Matt.9.37.}}

2328:
** θερμαίνω**,
_aorist_ ἐθέρμηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.7, etc., later ἐθέρμᾱνα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 730a16}}: _perfect_ τεθέρμαγκα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} see at {κεχλίαγκα}: _perfect_ _passive_ τεθέρμασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 147.4, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1573.47, (δια-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.64}}: (θερμός): —**warm, heat**, εἰς ὅ κε θερμὰ λοετρὰ.. Ἑκαμήδη θερμήνῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.7; ἥλιος θερμαίνων χθόνα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 679, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 505; τὸ χαλκίον θέρμαινε 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 108}}:—_middle_, **cause to be warmed**, τῇ ἐρωμένῃ χαλκία δύο ὕδατος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.406.37 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, **to be heated**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.376, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63d; τὸ θερμαῖνον ψύχεται ὑπὸ τοῦ θερμαινομένου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 768b18}}; **feel the sensation of heat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186d}}; **to be** or **grow feverish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26}}.ι; **to be parched**, of roots, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.11.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, θ. φιλότατι νόον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).87; ἕως ἐθέρμην᾽ αὐτὸν φλὸξ οἴνου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 758; σπλάγχν᾽ ἐθέρμαινον ποτῷ [prev. author] “Cyc.” 424; σπλάγχνα θ. κότῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 844}}; πολλὰ θερμαίνοι φρενί is probably _falsa lectio_ for{π. θ. φρένα}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 990(1004); οὐ τοῦτο μή σε θερμήνῃ 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.20:}} —_passive_, κεναῖσιν ἐλπίσιν θερμαίνεται **glows** with hope, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 478}}; χαρᾷ θ. καρδίαν **have** one's heart **warm** with joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 402.}}

2329:
** θέρμη**,
also **θέρμᾰ** (which see), ἡ, **heat**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19, LXX.Sir.38.28, NT.Act.28.3}}; τῆς θ. **when it is hot**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 98.20}}; **feverish heat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 158, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 178c (θερμά codices), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 862a18: 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.51, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.8.}}
**II** θέρμαι, αἱ, **hot springs,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.455 (from Catana), compare 1055: name of a town in Sicily, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.24.4.}}
**II.2** **hot baths,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 473.5 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

2330:
** θέρος**, εος, τό,
(θέρω) **summer**, χείματος οὐδὲ θέρευς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.118; οὔτ᾽ ἐν θέρει οὔτ᾽ ἐν ὀπώρῃ 12.76}}; ἐν θέρει, opposed to ἐνψύχει, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 18}}; θέρεϊ or θέρει, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.151, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 640}}; τὸ θέρος **during the summer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.202}}; τοῦ θέρεος **in the course of it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.24; τοῦ θέρους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 463}}; θέρεος or θέρους (without the _Article_), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276b, +others; τοῦ παρεστῶτος θέρους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1340; τοῦ θ. εὐθὺς ἀρχομένου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47; κατὰ θέρους ἀκμήν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.19}}; θ. μεσοῦντος **about** mid**summer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 1; especially in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus}}, **campaigning-season**, ἅμα ἦρι τοῦ ἐπιγιγνομένου θέρους {{Refs|4.117, compare 2.31, 6.8; τοσαῦτα μὲν ἐν τῷ θ. ἐγένετο 2.68.}}
**II** **summerfruits, harvest, crop**, θ. ἀλλότριον ἀμᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 392, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.21, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.365.3 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus): _plural_, θέρη}}**crops,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 150.5 (3rd c.AD)}}; θέρη σταχύων the **ripe** ears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 2: _metaphorically_, πάγκλαυτον ἐξαμᾷ θέρος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 822, compare “Ag.” 1655; τὸ γηγενὲς δράκοντος.. θ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1026}}; of a horse's mane, see at {θερίζω} {{Refs|I.3}}; of a youth's beard, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 298, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.19 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus); also τέμνεται τὸ ἱερὸν καὶ ἀπόρρητον θ. τοῦ θεοῦ Τάλλου 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.168d.}}
**III** Astron., τὸ μέγα θ., ὅταν πάντες οἱ πλάνητες ἐν θερινῷ ζῳδίῳ γένωνται {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 111.30.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, in an epitaph for a **year** of life, {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 2.874.4 (from Egypt).}}

2334:
** θεωρ-έω**:—
_passive_, _future_ **-ηθήσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.280}}: _future_ _middle_ in _passive_sense,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1.70, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 7.10}}: (θεωρός):—**to be a** θεωρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1 (which see), μαντεύεσθαι καὶ θ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.18; ἐγὼ δὲ τεθεώρηκα πώποτ᾽ οὐδαμοῖ πλὴν ἐς Πάρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1188}}; of the states which sent θεωροί, οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι ἐθεώρουν ἐς τὰ Ἴσθμια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.10.}}
**2** **to be sent to consult an oracle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 315b.}}
**II** of spectators at games, τὰ Ὀλύμπια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; ἀγῶνα [prev. author] 8.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.10}}; θ. τινά **to see** him **act**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 11.3: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.265}}; **to go as a spectator**, ἐς τὰ Ἐφέσια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104; ἐς Ὀλυμπίαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 50}}; see entry ὀβολός {{Refs|1.}}
**III** **look at, behold**, γῆν πολλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76; τύχας τινός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 304; τὰ περὶ τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467c}}; **inspect, review** soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.16, “HG” 4.5.6}}: _absolutely_, **gaze, gape**, ἑστηκὼς θ. [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.5: Astrology texts, ={ἐπιθεωρέω} 5, τὴν σελήνην 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.542.}}]
**III.2** of the mind, **contemplate, consider**, αὐτῇ τῇ ψυχῇ αὐτὴν τὴν ψυχὴν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 523e; τὰ ὄντα ᾗ ὄντα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1003b15, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 2.6, etc.}}: followed by an _interrogative_, τοῦτο θ., εἰ τἀληθῆ λέγω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.3; θ. τινά, ὁποτέρου τοῦ βίου ἐστίν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.168; πόσας ἔχουσι διαφοράς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 761a11; θ. τίνας λέγομεν τοὺς φρονίμους [prev. author] “EN” 1140a24}}; θ. τι ἔκ τινος **to judge of** one thing by another, τὴν ἔννοιαν ἐκ τῶν ἔργων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.13, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.160}}; θ. τι πρός τι **compare** one thing **with** another, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.17; πρὸς τοὺς πρὸ ἐμαυτοῦ.. κρίνωμαι καὶ θεωρῶμα; [prev. work]315; τοὺς πρέσβεις θ. πρὸς τὸν καιρὸν καθ᾽ ὃν ἐπρές βευον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.80}}; θ. [τι τεκμηρίοις] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.199.}}
**III.2.b** **observe**, θ. μᾶλλον τοὺς πέλας δυνάμεθα ἢ ἑαυτούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1169b33, compare “Pol.” 1263b25, +others; ταῦτα ἐμοῦ ἐθεωρήσατε, ὡς.. ποιουμένου 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 28}}:—_passive_, τεθεώρηται τοῦτο μάλιστα ἐπὶ τῶν περιστερῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 562a23, compare 540b19, +others}}; λόγῳ θεωρεῖσθαι, of objects not accessible to sense, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.10: _absolutely_, ὡς καὶ ἐπ᾽ ἄλλων θεωρεῖται [prev. work]1.13.}}
**III.2.c** **perceive**, with _infinitive_, ἀναγκαῖον ὑπάρχειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.88.}}
**III.2.d** _absolutely_, **speculate, theorize**, ἀκριβῶς, φορτικῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b28, “Metaphorical” 1001b14}}; λογικῶς, φυσικῶς, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 88a19, “Cael.” 304a25; περί τινος [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1004b1, 983a33 (_passive_)}}; θ. ἔκ τινος **to conclude by observation** from..,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1029a26; διά τινος [prev. author] “Mete.” 353b18:}} —_passive_, ἡ παρὰ τοῖς Ἕλλησι τεθεωρημένη μάθησις Ael.Tact.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Prooem.” (θεωρήσασα is probably corrupt for ἐωρ- in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1084 (Lyric poetry).)}}

2335:
** θεωρ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ θεωρίη, _Doric dialect_ **θεᾱρία** (see. below), _Boeotian dialect_ **θιαωρία** Ἐφ.Ἀρχ. 1892.34: ἡ:— **sending of** θεωροί or **state-ambassadors** to the oracles or games, or, collectively, **the** θεωροί **themselves, embassy, mission**, θεωρίαν ἀπάγειν εἰς Δῆλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58b: _plural_, opposed to στρατεῖαι, [prev. author] “R.” 556c}}; ἄγειν τῷ Διὶ τῷ Νεμείῳ τὴν κοινὴν ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.115, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.2, Decrees Byzantine cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.91 (θεᾱρία), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.19.4.}}
**2** **office of** θεωρός, **discharge of that office**, τῆς Ὀλυμπίαζε θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.10, etc.}}
**II** **being a spectator at the theatre** or **games**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1491; οὔτ᾽ ἐπὶ θεωρίαν πώποτε ἐκ τῆς πόλεως ἐξῆλθες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 52b; personified in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 523, +others}}
**III** **viewing, beholding**, θεωρίης εἵνεκεν ἐκδημεῖν to go abroad **to see the world**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30}}; κατὰ θεωρίης πρόφασιν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 29; ἐκπέμπειν τινὰ κατ᾽ ἐμπορίαν καὶ κατὰ θεωρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 11.1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.24}}; **pilgrimage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1047.}}
**III.2** of the mind, **contemplation, consideration**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 38b: _plural_, θεῖαι θ. [prev. author] “R.” 517d: with _genitive_, παντὸς μὲν χρόνου πάσης δὲ οὐσίας [prev. work] 486a}}; ἡ τῶν ἀρχῶν, ἡ τῶν ὅλων θ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.55U., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.288S.; θ. ποιεῖσθαι περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 989b25}}; ἡ περὶ φύσεως θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.3U., etc.: _plural_, τὰς σαθρὰς αὐτοῦ θ. Demetrius Astrologus Lac. “Herc.” 124.12.}}
**III.2.b** **theory, speculation**, opposed to practice, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.5.3}}; ἡ περὶ τὰ στρατόπεδα θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.42.6}}; αἱ νυκτεριναὶ καὶ ἡμεριναὶ θ. **theoretic reckoning** of night and day, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.14.6}}; ἡ μαθηματικὴ θ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 12.}}
**III.3** _passive_, **sight, spectacle**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 802, etc.}}; especially **public spectacle at the theatre** or **games**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1005, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.12}}; ἡ τοῦ Διονύσου θ. the Dionysia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 650a.}}
**III.4** Rhet., **explanatory preface** to a μελέτη, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor in “Hermes” 17.208, etc.}}: so in Philos., **continuous exposition**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 18.30, +others}}

2336:
** θήκ-η**, ἡ,
(τίθημι) **case, chest**, χρυσοῦ θ. a money-**chest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, 9.83, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1147, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.17, etc.}}
**2** **grave, tomb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 405, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1763 (_anapaest meter_); νεκρῶν θήκας ἀνοίγειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, compare 67, +others; αἱ θ. τῶν τεθνεώτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, 3.104}}; εἰς ἀναισχύντους θ. ἐτράποντο **modes of burial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427b; θήκην ὀρύττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.5.}}
**3** ξίφους θ. sword-**sheath**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.144}}; τόξου bow-**case,** {{Refs|EM 333.41.}}

2337:
** θηλάζω**,
_Doric dialect_ _aorist 1_ ἐθήλαξα [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.16 (variant{-αζε})}}]: (θηλή):
**I** of the mother or nurse, **suckle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 29, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 576b10}}: _absolutely_, **give suck**, οἱ μαστοί, οἳ οὐκ ἐθήλασαν {{NT|NT.Luke.23.29}}:—also in _middle_, ἐπιμελεῖσθαι, ὅπως μέτριον χρόνον θηλάσονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566b17; οὐ συλλαμβάνουσι θηλαζόμεναι [prev. author] “GA” 777a13, compare “IG” 5(2).514.12 (from Lycosura)}}:—_passive_, **to be sucked**, ὁ δελφὶς.. θηλάζεται ὑπὸ τῶν τέκνων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 504b25.}}
**II** of the young animal, **suck**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 733b29, etc.; ἐλέφαντος ὁ σκύμνος θ. τῷ στόματι [prev. author] “HA” 578a22}}; θηλάζων χοῖρος a **sucking** pig, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.15}}; seldom of an infant, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 49.87.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, λεαίνας μασδὸν ἐθήλαξεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.16; ἐὰν μὴ τύχῃ τεθηλακὼς ὁ ὄνος ἵππον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577b16. (Written θελάσζ- “PSI” 4.368.19 (3rd c.BC).)}}

2338:
** θῆλυς**, θήλεια, θῆλυ:
_Epic dialect_ _feminine_ θήλεα, _accusative_ _plural_ **-εας**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.269 (8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has regular _feminine_ θήλεια 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.7, +others, but also θῆλυς as _feminine_, 10.216, +others, as in other poets, see below): _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ θήλεα, θήλεαν, θηλέης, θηλέῃ, _plural_ θήλεαι, θηλέας, θηλέων, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus: _genitive_ θήλυδος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1054}}; _accusative_ _feminine_ θηλείην uncertain reading in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 42, _neuter_ _plural_ θήλεια 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1068: _Epic dialect_ also θηλύτερος indicating opposition rather than comparison (compare ἀρρέντερος); θηλύτεραι δὲ γυναῖκες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.520; θηλύτεραι δὲ θεαί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.324; μάτε ἐρσεναιτέραν μάτε θηλυτέραν “Schwyzer” 424 (Elis, 4th c.BC)}}; in late Prose θηλύτερος, -ύτατος occur as _comparative_ and _superlative_ (see. below 11): (θη- 'suckle', compare θῆσαι):—**female**, θήλεια θεός a goddess, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.7}}; Ἥρη θῆλυς ἐοῦσα being **female**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.97, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1231, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1062, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 621}}; θήλειαι ἵπποι mares, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.636, etc.; σύες θήλειαι sows, 14.16}}; ὄϊς θῆλυς a ewe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.216; θήλεια μῆλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1068}}; θήλεια ἔλαφος a hind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.29; θήλεα κάμηλος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102}}; ἡ θ. ἵππος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 86; θ. ὄρνις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 477}}; ζῷα θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 110c}}; ἄπαις θήλεος γόνου without **female** issue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.66; θῆλυς σπορά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 659; θήλειαι γυναῖκες [prev. author] “Or.” 1205; θ. κόραι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 764d: with _masculine_ nouns, ὁ θῆλυς ὀρεύς}} the **she**-mule, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 577b22; ἄνθρωπος θῆλυς [prev. author] “PA” 688b31: _masculine_ _plural_, θήλεις χοροί 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 1.8D.; but μὴ εἶναι θεοὺς ἄρρενας μηδὲ θηλείας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 12.}}
**b** ἡ θήλεα, _Attic dialect_ -εια, **the female**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.4; ἀλέκτωρ ὥστε θηλείας πέλας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1671 (troch.).}}
**c** τὸ θ. γένος the **female** sex, **woman**-kind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 885}}; τὸ θ. alone, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 536, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ ἄρρεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 454d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 988a5; [ἡ δεῖνα] τέτοκεν θῆλυ “PTeb.” 422.18 (3rd c.AD), +others}}
**d** of plants and trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.1; θ. κάλαμος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.85; θῆλυς φοῖνιξ 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.17; θῆλυ βούτομον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.10.4.}}
**2** **of** or **belonging to women**, κουράων θῆλυς ἀϋτή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.122}}; θήλεα νοῦσος among the Scythians (compare Ἐνάρεες), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.105; νόμος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 821 (Lyric poetry); φύσις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 453a; χάρις “APl.” 4.87 (6th c.AD: Leontius Epigrammaticus)}}; θ. φόνος murder **by women**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 796.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of persons and things,
**II.1** **soft, gentle**, θῆλυς ἐέρση {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.467, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 395}}; θ. νύξ (={ὕπομβρος}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1053.}}
**II.1.b** ὕδωρ θ. καὶ μαλακόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.6.3; θηλυτέρα ὀσμή [prev. work] 6.15.4}}; θηλύτατον πεδίον **most fruitful**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 296}}; θηλύτατον ὕδωρ of the Nile, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sos.” vii 5.}}
**II.2** **tender, delicate**, Φοίβου θήλειαι.. παρειαί {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 37}}; θῆλυς ἀπὸ χροιῆς **delicate** of skin, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.49}}; of temper or character, **soft, yielding, weak**, θῆλυς ηὕρημαι τάλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1075; γυνὴ δὲ θῆλυ κἀπὶ δακρύοις ἔφυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 928; θήλεια φρήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 708, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 181; δίαιτα θηλυτέρα ἢ κατ᾽ ἄνδρα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 34; θηλύτατος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 13; παλλακὴ -υτάτη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.21.2}}; τὸ θῆλυ τῆς ψυχῆς **effeminacy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 599.}}
**II.3** in mechanics, those parts were called female **into which others fitted**, as the **female** vertebra, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.180; γίγγλυμος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.6.3.}}
**II.4** Grammars, **feminine**, θήλεα [ὀνόματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 682; θήλεα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1458a10.}}
**II.5** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}}, of **even** numbers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.264a, 288d.}}
**II.6** Astrology texts, of planets, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 19; compare θηλυκός 3c.}}
**III** θήλειαι, αἱ, kind of cheese made in Crete, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Seleucus Grammaticus et Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.650d.}}

2339:
** θήρα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θήρη**, ἡ, **hunting of wild beasts, the chase**, βάν ῥ᾽ ἴμεν ἐς θήρην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.429; αἵμονα θήρης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.49; ἰέναι ἐπὶ τὴν θήρην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37, 4.114, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; ζῶσι ἀπὸ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.22, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1256a35; ἐποίησε μεγάλην θήραν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14; θ. ποιεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 541a20; τὰς θ. τῶν ὀρτύγων ἐποιοῦντο 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.60}}; τοῦ πτηνοῦ γένους θ., ={ὀρνιθευτική}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 220b}}; ἡ περὶ θάλατταν θ. **fishing**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 823d; κυνηγεσία καὶ ἡ ἄλλη θ. [prev. work] 763b: _plural_, πέρδικες εἰς τὰς θ. ἀγόμεναι, of decoy birds, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 751a14, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.42 W., 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 41.2.}}
**b** in Ptolemaic Egypt, στρατηγὸς ἐπὶ τὴν θ. τῶν ἐλεφάντων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 82, 86 (3rd c.BC),compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4.5, 7, Wilcken “Chr.” 385.14 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.292 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **eager pursuit** of anything, θήραν.. ἔχομεν τόξων, ={θηρῶμεν τὰ τόξα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 840; δυσμενῶν θήραν ἔχειν [prev. author] “Aj.” 564; θ. ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 222b, 222c; τοῦ ἡδέος [prev. author] “Grg.” 500d; ἐπιστημῶν [prev. author] “Tht.” 198a, etc.}}
**II** **prey, game**, αἶψα δ᾽ ἔδωκε θεὸς μενοεικέα θήρην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.158, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 251, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1144}}; πρὶν κινεῖσθαι τὴν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.25}}; θήραν καλήν, of a prisoner, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 609: in _plural_, ὦ πταναὶ θῆραι, of birds, [prev. work] 1146 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὴν θ. ἐπὶ τοῦ μέσου τηροῦσα watching its **prey**, of a spider, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623a13.}}
**III** **hunting-ground, preserve**, Ἁδριανοῦ θῆραι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.10.}}
**IV** in Roman times, **the games of the Circus,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 351.3 (from Nicaea).}}

2340:
** θηρ-εύω**,
_aorist 1_ ἐθήρευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 290c}}: _perfect_ τεθήρευκα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 200a}}:—_middle_, _future_ **-σομαι**{{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 222a}}: _aorist_ ἐθηρευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197d:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ τεθήρευμαι Lysipp.Comedy texts {{Refs|7}}: _aorist_ ἐθηρεύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 493, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 221a: (compare θηράω)}}:—**hunt**, θηρεύοντα **while hunting**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.465, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112}}; θηρεύειν διὰ κενῆς, of the motions of the hands of dying persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 4.}}
**2** **decoy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 614a13.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **hunt after, chase, catch**, ἀττελέβους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172}}; θηρία, ὄρνιθας ἀγρίας, μῦν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197c, “PCair.Zen.” 300.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὶχθῦς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 603a7}}; [ἐλέφαντας] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 54.11 (Adule, 3rd c.BC)}}; of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.183; θ. ἀνθρώπους ἐπὶ θοίνην ἤ θυσίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1324b39, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.13}}; Τιτυὸν βέλος θήρευσε it **hit, struck** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.90}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464d, “Euthd.” 290b}}:—_passive_, **to be hunted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102}}; **to be preyed upon**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 108}}; **to be caught**, πέδαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 493}}: _metaphorically_, **to be captivated**, Lysipp.Comedy texts {{Refs|7.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **hunt, seek after**, κερδέων μέτρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.47; γάμους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 858; ἀρετάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 568 (Lyric poetry); θ. νέους πλουσίους ὀρφανούς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.170}}; ἡδονάς, ἐπιστήμην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200a, +others}}; [εὐδαιμονίαν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1328b1}}; ὀνόματα, ῥήματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 489b, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.18; τὰς ἀρχὰς τῶν συλλογισμῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 46a11}}; θ. τὸν πλησίον, of an orator, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.5 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others}} —Trag. preferred θηράω, except where metre demanded θηρεύω.

2342:
** θηρίον**, τό
(in form _diminutive_ of θήρ), **wild animal**, especially of such as are hunted, μάλα γὰρ μέγα θηρίον ἦεν, of a stag, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.171, 180 (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}}; in Trag. only in Satyric drama, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 147 (uncertain in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 26): used in Prose for θήρ, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.163, etc.}}; of the spider's **prey**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623a27}}; frequently of elephants, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.1.12, +others}}: _plural_, **beasts**, opposed to men, birds, and fishes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Venerem” 4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108.}}
**2** generally, **animal**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.119; νενόμισται πῦρ θ. εἶναι ἔμψυχον [prev. author] 3.16}}; of men, ἄνθρωπος πάντων θ. θεειδέστατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 48; εἰς θηρίου βίον ἀφικνεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249b; also θ. ὕειον [prev. author] “R.” 535e}}; of the dog, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.79}}; of fishes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 598b1}}; of eels, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 147.7}}; of leeches, {{Refs|“IG” 4.951.101 (Epid.)}}; of other small creatures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 552b11, 625b32, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.103, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 19.6; οὐκ ἔστιν οὐδὲν θ. τῶν ἰχθύων ἀτυχέστερον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 161.1; opposed to plants, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188b: proverbial, ἢ θηρίον ἢ θεός}}, either above or below the nature of man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253a29, compare “EN” 1145a25.}}
**3** **beast**, especially as hostile and odious to man, θηρία τε καὶ βοτά **carnivora** and graminivora, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237d}}; **monster, creature**, of sharks, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44}}; of Typhon, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230a, “R.” 588e}}; of the Satyrs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” [same place]}}; ταυτὶ ποδαπὰ τὰ θ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 184, compare “Av.” 93.}}
**3.b** **poisonous animal**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.75, NT.Act.28.4.}}
**II** in Medicine texts,= θηρίωμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 459, “Loc.Hom.” 29, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus [prev. cited]}}
**III** as a term of reproach, **beast, creature**, ὦ δειλότατον σὺ θηρίον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 439, compare “Eq.” 273; κόλακι, δεινῷ θηρίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240b; Κρῆτες, κακὰ θ. 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 1}}; δυσνουθέτητον θ., of poverty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 78; ἡ μουσικὴ ἀεί τι καινὸν θηρίον τίκτει 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 27, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 132}}; τί δέ, εἰ αὐτοῦ τοῦ θηρίου ἠκούσατ; said by Aeschines of Demosthenes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Ep.” 2.3.10}}; θ. συνεστιώμενον, of woman, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 8.}}
**IV** Astron., the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Lupus”, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.20, Vett. Val. 6.13.}}

2343:
** θησαυρ-ίζω**,
**store, treasure up**, ἐν ἀσφαλείῃ τὰ χρήματα θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121}}.; θ. τὸν νεκρὸν ἐν οἰκήματι **to lay** it **by**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 86}}; φάρμακα, σῖτα θ. παρ᾽ αὑτῷ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.24, etc.}}; of fruits, **lay up in store, preserve, pickle**, [καυλοὺς] ἐν ἅλμῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.4.10}}; τὸ ἔλαιον θ. [τὰς ὀσμάς] **preserves** its smell, {{Refs|[prev. author] “CP” 6.19.3}}:—_passive_, ῥὰξ εὖ τεθησαυρισμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 398.2; [ἡ ἐβένη] τὴν χρόαν οὐ -ομένη λαμβάνει τὴν εὔχρουν ἀλλ᾽ εὐθὺς τῇ φύσει 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.6, compare 3.12.5; ἡ τεθησαυρισμένη τῶν ἀρωμάτων ἀπόλαυσις 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 97; τὸ θησαυρισθέν “IG” 14.423 ii 37 (from Tauromenium).}}
**b** _absolutely_, **hoard, lay up treasure**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.71J., NT.Jam.5.3; ἑαυτῷ NT.Luke.12.21.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, θ. σεαυτῷ ὀργήν {{NT|NT.Rom.2.5; θ. θησαυροὺς ἐν οὐρανῷ NT.Matt.6.20}}; θ. εὐτυχίαν **lay up a store of.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Σαυνιτική” 4.3}}:—_middle_, **store up for oneself**, ἑαυτῷ ὑπομνήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.229}}:—_passive_, τεθησαυρισμένος κατά τινος φθόνος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.36; χάριτας -ισθησομένας [prev. author] 1.90}}; **to be reserved**, πυρί {{NT|NT.2Pet.3.7.}}

2344:
** θησαυρός**, ὁ,
**store, treasure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 599, etc.}}; θ. χθονός, of the silvermines of Laureion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 238 (troch.); θ. εὑρεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b35}}; ἄνθρακες ὁ θ., proverbial, 'apples of Sodom', frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Zeux.” 2, +others; σποδὸς οἱ θ. γενήσονται 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.3.13: _metaphorically_, θ. γλώσσης φειδωλῆς 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 719; θ. ὕμνων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.8; κακῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 923, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Legal”}}; κόμας.., ἱκτήριον θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1175}}; Διὸς θ., of a tomb marking the fall of a thunderbolt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1010}}; οἰωνοῖς γλυκὺν θ., of a dead body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 30}}; of learning, θ., οὓς κατέλιπον ἐν βιβλίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.14}}; σοφίας θ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15e, NT.Col.2.3; χρημάτων καὶ τιμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 247b; καλὸς θ. παρ᾽ ἀνδρὶ σπουδαίῳ χάρις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.29; ἐκ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ θ. τῆς καρδίας NT.Luke.6.45.}}
**II** **strong-room, magazine**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150, “SIG” 419.17 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), LXX.Deu.32.34, etc.}}; especially of the **treasuries** built at Delphi by Greek cities, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 8 (6th c.BC), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.3.5, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 4.1.13, etc.}}; **vaults** of a bank, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 62ii 14 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.2** **granary,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 232.4 (3rd c.BC), Wilcken “Chr.” 385.27 (3rd c.BC), 192 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; οἱ δημόσιοι θ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 90.9 (3rd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 2119.3 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **receptacle** for valuables, **safe, casket**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.190, 9.106, NT.Matt.2.11}}; θ. βελέεσσιν, of a quiver, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1022 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.4** **offertory-box** (for its form, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).590), “IG” 7.235.23 (Oropus, 4th c.BC), 12(3).443 (Thera, 3rd c.BC), “Jahrb.” 16.162 note 13 (Rhodes, 3rd c.BC), “Schwyzer” 89 (Argos, 3rd c.BC), “SIG” 1015.30 (Halic.), “PTeb.” 6.27 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.89 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; σπονδεῖον ἢ θ. **coin-in-the-slot machine** which sold holy water, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.21.}}
**II.5** **cavern**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 276}}; **subterranean dungeon**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 19.}}

2345:
** θιγγάνω**,
_future_ προσ-θίξῃ probably to be read instead of **-εις**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 652: 2}} _future_ τεθίξομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1086}}: _aorist 2_ ἔθῐγον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 304, etc.}}; _Laconian dialect_ _infinitive_ σιγῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1004}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ θιχθῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.258}}:—**touch, handle**: _absolutely_, μὴ θίγγανε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).451 (from Thera), etc.: usually with _genitive_, οἴακος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 663, etc.}}; χερσὶ or χερὶ θ. τινός, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 44, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1318; δι᾽ ὁσίων χειρῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 470: with _accusative_, χεῖρα (assuming variant) Νεοβούλης θιγεῖν 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus [same place]}}; χερσὶ γλαυκᾶς ἐλαίας θιγοῦ[σ᾽ ὄζον] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Limenius Lyricus 6 (uncertain reading)}}:—_passive_, **to be touched**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 495a6.}}
**2** **take hold of**, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1410 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; θ. ὠλέναισιν τέκνου **embrace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 300 (Lyric poetry)}}; θ. γυναικός **have intercourse with.. **, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 1044}}; θ. εὐνῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 885, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 329: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 51}}; γλίχεται θιγεῖν καὶ συνεῖναι, of man's aspiration after God, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.1.}}
**3** **touch, attempt**, παντὸς.. λόγου κακοῦ γλώσσῃ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 408; μή μοι λεπτῶν θίγγανε μύθων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 924; θ. πονηρίας 3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 5}}; in hostile sense, **attack**, θηρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1183 (Lyric poetry); σώματος τοῦ σοῦ [prev. author] “IA” 1351 (troch.).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of the feelings, **touch**, θιγγάνει σέθεν τόδ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 310}}; ψυχᾶς, φρενῶν, {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus108}}; πολλὰ θ. πρὸς ἧπαρ **reach** to the heart, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 432 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **touch upon**, in speaking or discussion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 988a23, +others, “Pol.” 1323b38}}; also of the mind, **apprehend**, νοῦς.. θιγγάνων καὶ νοῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1072b21, +others}}
**II.3** **reach, win**, ἀγώνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.18, etc. also with _dative_, θ. ἡσυχίᾳ, ἀρεταῖς, ψεύδει, [prev. author] “P.” 4.296, 8.24, 9.42}}; **reach, hit**, διαβολὴ θ. τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 10.—Not found in pure _Attic dialect_ Prose (ἅπτομαι being used), but used by 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.5, +others:}}_aorist 1_ _infinitive_ θίξαι variant for{ψαῦσαι} in Suid. see at {θιγεῖν, θῖξαι} variant for{δεῖξαι} in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1111a14.}}

2346:
** θλῐβ-ω** [ῑ],
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1239, etc.:}}_future_ θλίψω (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 237}}: _aorist_ ἔθλιψα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60c, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 35}}: _perfect_ τέθλῐφα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Crobylus Comicus 4 (conjecture), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.24.3:}} —_middle_, _future_ θλίψομαι (see. below):—_passive_, _future_ θλῐβήσομαι variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.33}}: _aorist 1_ ἐθλίφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 925b20:}}_aorist 2_ _participle_ θλῐβείς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 13, variant in 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.7}} (compare _subjunctive_ ἐκφλῐβῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 9)}}: _perfect_ τέθλιμμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 925b14, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.472.5 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}:— **squeeze, chafe**, θλίβει τὸν ὄρρον [ὁ θώραξ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1239, compare “Lys.” 314; τοὺς ὄφεις θλίβων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.260}}; ὅπου με θλίβει where [the shoe] **pinches**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141a: _metaphorically_, δούλης ὦτα νωθρίη θλίβει 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.53}}: _absolutely_, **exercise pressure**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.6}}:—_passive_, of a person heavy-laden, ὡς θλίβομαι! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 5, compare “V.” 1289}}:—_middle_, πολλῇς φλιῇσι παραστὰς θλίψεται (variant{φλίψεται}) ὤμους he **will rub his** shoulders against many doorposts, of a beggar, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.221: χείλεα θλίβειν, of kissing, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.4.}}
**II** **compress, straiten**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60c}}; **reduce, compress**, εἰς τὸ μὴ ὂν τὰ ὄντα θλίβοντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.16U.}}:—_passive_, **to be compressed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 91a; ὥστε ἐξωθεῖσθαι τὸ ἧσσον θλιβόμενον ὑπὸ τοῦ μᾶλλον θλιβομένου 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 1 “Prooem.”}}; θλιβομένα καλύβα **a small, close** hut, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.18}}; ὁδὸς τεθλιμμένη, opposed to εὐρύχωρος, {{NT|NT.Matt.7.14}}; βίος τεθλ. a **scanty** subsistence, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.73, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.472.5 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **oppress, afflict, distress**, ἀνάγκη ἔθλιψέ τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 35; θ. καὶ λυμαίνεσθαι τὸ μακάριον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1100b28; θ. τὰς πόλεις τοῖς ὀψωνίοις “SIG” 700.25 (Macedonia, 2nd c.BC)}}; **press hard** in battle, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.24.3}}:— _passive_, θ. διὰ τὸν πόλεμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307a1; ὑπό τινων “SIG” 685.39 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὑπὸ τῆς ἀδοξίας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.61 O.—Once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, never in Trag.}}

2347:
** θλῖψις**, εως, ἡ,
**pressure**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 382a13, “Pr.” 890a2; τῶν νεφῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2 P. 49 U.}}; ἀντέρεισις καὶ θ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.3.6}}; of the pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.24.61, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.306; θ. στομάχου 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 42}}; ὑστερικαὶ θ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.42.}}
**2** **crushing, castration**, πώλων {{Refs|“Hippiatrica” 20.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **oppression, affliction**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Gen.35.3, +others, “BGU” 1139.4 (1st c.BC), NT.Act.14.22 (_plural_), +others, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 71.16 (_plural_), “POxy.” 939.13 (4th c.AD).}}

2348:
** θνῄσκω**
(with ι {{Refs|“IG” 2.2477.7, 10,2494, Ἀρχ.Ἐφ.1910.73}}; θνείσκ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 2.4040b; [ἀποθν]ήισκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” in “PPetr.” 1p.18 (3rd c.BC), but θνήσκω 1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus cited in “Etymologicum Magnum” 452.29, frequently in codices)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **θναίσκω** Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|2.79, _Doric dialect_}}**θνᾴσκω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 6754.22 (3rd c.BC)}}: _future_ θᾰνοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 85.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 462, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-έεσθαι**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.12; later θνήξομαι “Anthologia Graeca” 9.354 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.2.22 codices:}}_aorist 2_ ἔθᾰνον, _Epic dialect_ θάνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.412, +others}}; _infinitive_ _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ θανέειν, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, except 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.52, θανέμεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.72}}: _perfect_ τέθνηκα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.12, etc.}}; _subjunctive_ τεθνήκω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.74}}: _pluperfect_ ἐτεθνήκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.70, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.48}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ **-ήκεσαν**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.52}}: short forms of _perfect_, _3rd.pers._ _dual_ τέθνᾰτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.19}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ τέθνᾰμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493a}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ τεθνᾶσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.52, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _pluperfect_ ἐτέθνᾰσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.59, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.16; _imperative_ τέθνᾰθι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.365, τεθνάτω 15.496, “IG” 12.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933e, etc.; _optative_ τεθναίην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.98, etc.}}; _infinitive_ τεθνάναι [ᾰ] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1012, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 68, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, etc., τεθνᾶναι}} uncertain reading in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.10, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 539}}; _Epic dialect_ τεθνάμεναι, -άμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.225, 15.497, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ τεθνάκην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15}}; _participle_ τεθνεώς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 476, etc., _feminine_ τεθνεῶσα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.22, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.27 (τεθνηκυῖα 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 29, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 109), _neuter_ τεθνεός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 32 (τεθνηκός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71d, _plural_ τεθνεῶτα 72c)}}; _genitive_ τεθνεῶτος, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.68, etc. (once in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _dative_ τεθνεῶτι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.331)}}; _poetry_ τεθνεότος Archel. cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 89, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 7.65}}; _Doric dialect_ τεθνᾰότα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.74}}; _Epic dialect_ τεθνηώς (variant{-ειώς}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.161}}, **-ηυῖα** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.734, (κατα-) 11.141}}; _genitive_ τεθνηῶτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.633, etc.; also τεθνηότος 17.435, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.23, +others [τεθνεῶτι}} is _trisyllable_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.331, τεθνεώτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 272 (hexameters)}}: _disyllable_ forms are written in later Gr., _nominative_ τεθνώς {{Refs|“BCH” 18.438 (from Argilus)}}; _genitive_ _singular_ τεθνῶτος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 799.13 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD)}}; _dative_ _singular_ τεθνῶτι {{Refs|“Papers of the Amer. School” 3.334 (Pisid.)}}; _feminine_ τεθνώσῃ (and _genitive_ _plural_ τεθνήτων) {{Refs|“Ath.Mitt.” 50.134 (from Macedonia)}}; _accusative_ _plural_ _feminine_ τεθνώσας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 45.9]: from τέθνηκα}} arose _future_ τεθνήξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1279}} (censured as archaic by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 7), later τεθνήξομαι 2nd c.BC(?): Diogenianus Epicureus 1.28, 3.52, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 10, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.46}}; _participle_ τεθνηξόμενος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 438.7.}}—The simple Verb is regularly used in early Prose in _perfect_ and _pluperfect_; for _present_, _future_, and _aorist_ the _compound_ ἀποθνῄσκω is substituted: θνῄσκει variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.9, σάρκες θνῄσκουσι “Art.” 69, ἔθνῃσκον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47, +others, θνῃσκόντων [prev. work] 53, θνῄσκοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72d, θνῄσκομεν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.20U.}}: _aorist_ _participle_ θανών, _subjunctive_ θάνῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).593.2,20,23 (Iulis, 5th c.BC), “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.166 (from Cyrene), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1649.4}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ θανεῖν {{Refs|[prev. work] 1418.13}}:—in _present_ and _imperfect_, **die**, as well of natural as of violent death; in _aorist_ and _perfect_, **to be dead** (compare τί τοὺς θανὅντας οὐκ ἐᾷς τεθνηκένα; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 12.3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; τέθνηκ᾽ ἔγωγε πρὶν θανεῖν κακῶν ὕπο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 431), θανεῖν καὶ πότμον ἐπισπεῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.52, etc.}}; ζωὸς ἠὲ θανών alive or **dead**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.553, compare 15.350; ἦ ἤδη τέθνηκε 4.834; ὡς ἄμεινον εἴη τεθνάναι μᾶλλον ἢ ζώειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, compare 7.46; τεθνάναι κρεῖττον ἤ.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.65, compare 10.25; ἄξιος τεθνάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1012, etc.}}; τεθνάτω **let** him **be put to death,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.10.29}}; ἄτιμος τεθ. Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.44}}: frequently in _participle_, νέκυος πέρι τεθνηῶτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.173}}; νεκρὸν.. τεθνηῶτα a **dead** corpse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.10}}; οἱ τεθνηκότες, οἱ θανόντες, **the dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 278, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus [same place], etc.; οὔτε τεθνεῶτα οὔτε ζῶντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14}}; οἴχεται θανών (see. οἴχομαι); θανὼν φροῦδος (see. φροῦδος); θανόντι συνθανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 798, “fragment” 953, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1007 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ θανών, opposed to ὁ κτανών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 336:}}_present_ with _perfect_ sense, θνῄσκουσι γάρ, for τεθνήκασι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 118, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 695 (Lyric poetry), “Ba.” 1041 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**2** used like a _passive_ Verb, χερσὶν ὑπ᾽ Αἴαντος θανέειν **to fall** by his hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.289; θ. ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 329c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625a16; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.72, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1454}}; πρός τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 292, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 773; θεοῖς τέθνηκε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 970: frequently with _dative_ instrumenti, θ. χερί, δορί, [prev. author] “OC” 1388, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 959 (Lyric poetry); φαρμάκοισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fk.” 464; also ἐν βρόχῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 558}}; τεθνάναι τῷ δέει, τ. τῷ φόβῳ, with _accusative_, **to be in mortal fear of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.45, 19.81, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.9.4; προοίμιον σκοτεινὸν καὶ τεθνηκὸς δειλίᾳ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.34}}; θ. ἐπί τινι **to die** leaving one as heir, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 7.1.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, of things, **perish**, θνᾴσκει σιγαθὲν καλὸν ἔργον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 121; ἐσλῶν ὑπὸ χαρμάτων πῆμα θνᾴσκει.. δαμασθέν [prev. author] “O.” 2.19; λόγοι θνῄσκοντες μάτην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 846; θ. πίστις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 611; τὸ τρύβλιον τέθνηκέ μοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 986 (Lyric poetry): in Prose, τέθνηκε τὸ τοὺς ἀδικοῦντας μισεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.289; τεθνηκός τι φθέγξασθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.54; τεθνηκὸς ὁρᾶν 4th c.AD: Callistratus Sophista “Statuarum descriptiones” 14; τὸ τεθνηκὸς ὁ λίθος ὑπεδύετο [prev. work] 2.}}

2349:
** θνητός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 973, “IA” 901, 1396}}: _Doric dialect_ **θνᾱτός** (see. below): _Aeolic dialect_ **θνᾶτος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 13.7}}: (θνῄσκω):— **liable to death, mortal**, opposed to ἀθάνατος, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.213, +others; θ. ἄνδρες 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 967; οὐδὲν.. θνητὸν ἐόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.98; ζῷα πάντα θ. καὶ φυτά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 265c: as}} _substantive_, θνητοί **mortals**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.593, etc.}}; θνηταί **mortal women**, {{Refs|5.213}}; πάντων τῶν θ. of all **mortal creatures**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.216, 2.68}}; εἴ τις φθόγγος (φθόγγον codex, but θ. is only used of living persons) εἰσακούεται θνητῶν παρ᾽ Ἅιδῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 491}}: _comparative_, ἐν θνητῷ ὄντες, ἔτι θνητοτέρους ἑαυτοὺς ποιοῦντες {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.20}}: _superlative_, θνητότατος πάντων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.1.}}
**2** of things, **befitting mortals, human**, ἔργματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1069; θνατὰ θνατοῖσι πρέπει 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).16; θνατὰ χρὴ τὸν θνατὸν.. φρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [263], compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 473; τὸ δαιμόνιον μεταξύ ἐστι θεοῦ τε καὶ θνητοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202e.}}

2350:
** θορῠβ-έω**,
**make a noise, uproar** or **disturbance**, especially of crowds, assemblies, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 12, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 666, “V.” 622, etc.; βλέπων εἰς τὸν ἀεὶ θορυβοῦντα τόπον τῆς ἐκκλησίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.194.}}
**2** **shout in token of approbation** or **the contrary**:
**2.a** **cheer, applaud**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.264, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 303b:}} —_passive_, λόγος τεθορυβημένος a **loudly cheered** speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.233, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1356b23.}}
**2.b** more frequently **raise clamour**, καί μοι μὴ θορυβήσητε pray **do** not **interrupt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20e, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.15; θ. ἐφ᾽ οἷς ἂν λέγω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30c}}; ὁ θορυβῶν, opposed to ὁ θέλων λέγειν καὶ ἀκούειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.7}}:—_passive_, **have clamours raised against one**, ὑπὸ τοιούτων ἀνδρῶν θορυβῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 164 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** _transitive_, **confuse by noise** or **tumult, bewilder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245b, “Prt.” 319c, +others}}; **throw** [troops] **into confusion**, in battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.78}}; θ. πρός τινας **cause excitement** amongst.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.61}}:—_passive_, **to be thrown into disorder, confused**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, 4.130, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.129, 8.50, Pl “Ep.” 348e, etc.; ὑπὸ τῶν λεγομένων [prev. author] “Ly.” 210e}}; τινι at a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.35; ἐπί τινι 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 7.2; περί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.61; πρός τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 29.}}

2351:
** θόρῠβ-ος**, ὁ,
**noise**, especially **the confused noise of a crowded assembly, uproar, clamour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).72, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92, etc.}}; θόρυβος βοῆς a confused clamour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1263; θ. στρατιωτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 546; θ. Πυκνίτης “Comica Adespota” 45D.; θ. παρέχειν ἐν ταῖς βουλαῖς καὶ ἐν ταῖς ἐκκλησίαις “OGI” 48.9 (Ptolemais, 3rd c.BC)}}; less frequently of an individual, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 905}}; of animals, θόρυβον δ᾽ οὐκ ἐφίλησαν ὄνων {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2079.30.}}
**2** especially in token of approbation or the contrary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 492b, 492c:}}
**2.a** **applause**, θ. Ληναΐτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 547; θ. καὶ ἔπαινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.195; θόρυβον καὶ κρότον ἐποιήσατε [prev. author] 21.14.}}
**2.b** **groans, murmurs**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.15}}; μεγάλοι θόρυβοι κατέχουσ᾽ ἡμᾶς great **murmurs** are abroad among us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** **tumult, confusion**, θ. παρασχεῖν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.181}}; ἐς θ. ἀπικέσθαι, καταστῆναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.104; ἐγένετο ὁ θ. μέγας, in a battle, [prev. work] 14}}; κραυγὴ καὶ θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον ἀγαθοῦ βασιλέως” p.22 O.: _plural_, θ. ὀχλώδεις καὶ παροινίαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 239.}}
**II.2** **confusion** of mind, θορύβους ἐνθυμηματικοὺς καὶ ἀποφθεγματικοὺς παρασκευάζειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.9}}; ὁ παρὰ κακὰς δόξας θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.31 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 40S.}}
**III** with _infinitive_, ἐς θόρυβον ἤλυθον.. λευσθῆναι I ran a **risk** of being stoned, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1349 (troch.). (Perh. _cognate_ with τονθορύζω.)}}

2352:
** θραύω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ θραύεσκον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 140}}: _future_ **-σω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 466}}: _aorist 1_ ἔθραυσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 745, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 779 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, _future_ θραυσθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.624:}}_aorist_ ἐθραύσθην (see. below), (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56e}}: _perfect_ τέθραυσμαι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 11, (συν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.14, (παρα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757e (variant{-τεθραυμένον})}}]:—**break in pieces, shatter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 57, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 196, 416, 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 99, etc.; θ. σάρκας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1239:}} —_passive_, θραυομένης τῆς πέτρης **flying into pieces**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.174; σίδηρον θραυσθέντα καὶ ῥαγέντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 476}}; πτερὰ θραύονται **have their** wings **broken**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248b.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **break down, enfeeble**, μὴ θραύσαι (-σοι codices) χρόνος ὄλβον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.97, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 779 (Lyric poetry); διάτορον σφραγῖδα θ. στόματος 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 160; ἔπος.., ὅ τι τὴν τούτων θραύσει ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 466; θ. τὴν δύναμιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 23}}; ἐλπίδα, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.2.2, etc.; θ. τι τῶν ἐκ χρόνου φυλασσομένων δικαίων “Supp.epigram” 1.329.45 (Istrus, 1st c.AD),compare 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 32.10}}:—_passive_, πόθος θραυσθείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aspasius Philosophus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.219e}}; θραυόμενος τὸν λογισμόν, Latin _animo fractus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 17}}; θραυσθῆναι ἐπί τινα **to be grieved** for.., {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.20.34.—Rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose.}}

2353:
** θρέμμ-α**, ατος, τό,
(τρέφω) **nursling, creature**, θ. Νηρεΐδων, of dolphins, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Arion Lyricus 1.9}}; mostly of tame animals, especially sheep and goats, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 9.6, “Oec.” 20.23, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.26.5, NT.John.4.12, etc.}}; τὰ ἐν ταῖς ἀγέλαις θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 261d; τὰ ἀγελαῖα θ. [prev. work] 264a}}; ὑηνὰ θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 819d}}; of game-cocks and quails, ὀρνίθων θ{{Refs|. [prev. work] 789b}}: generally, **animals**, τοῖς ἡμέροις καὶ ἀγρίοις.. θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 118b, +others}}
**2** of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1143, “Ph.” 243}}; Χαρίτων θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 973; δύσκολον τὸ θ. ἄνθρωπος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777b, compare “Tht.” 174b}}; especially of **domestic slaves**,= Latin _verna_, τὸ Χρυσίππου θ. {{Refs|“GDI” 12321.14 (Delph.), compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3113 (from Teos).}}
**3** generally, **creature**, ἄπλατον θ. κἀπροσήγορον, of a lion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1093 (compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155e)}}; of Cerberus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1099}}; κακὰ θ., of **a swarm** of gnats, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.150 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}; θ. Σελινοῦντος, of a fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 12}}; Καρύστου θ., comic for a cup made at Carystus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 182.3}}; as a term of reproach, θρέμματ᾽ οὐκ ἀνασχετά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 182; ὦ θρέμμ᾽ ἀναιδές 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 622, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 369}}; in _periphrastic_, ὕδρας θ., for ὕδρα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 574; νεογενῆ παίδων θρέμματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 790d}}; θρέμματα παλλακῶν **kept** mistresses, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 7. (Written θέρματα “BGU” 478.15 (2nd c.AD)).}}

2354:
** θρην-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1541 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 632 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist 1_ **-ησα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” [111]}}:—_middle_ (see. below {{Refs|2)}}: _impersonal_ in _perfect_ _passive_ (see. below): (θρῆνος):—**sing a dirge, wail**, Μοῦσαι δ᾽ ἐννέα πᾶσαι ἀμειβόμεναι ὀπὶ καλῇ θρήνεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.61}}; τίς ὁ θρηνήσω; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.”}}[same place]; τίς.. ἔσθ᾽ ὁ θρηνῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1260; θ. πρὸς τύμβον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 926; θ. καὶ ὀδύρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 38d; πρὸς σφᾶς αὐτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.128: c._accusative_ _cognate_, στονόεσσαν ἀοιδὴν.. ἐθρήνεον}} **were singing** a doleful dirge, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.7221}}; γόον θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 291; ὀξυτόνους ᾠδάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” [same place]; ἐπῳδάς [prev. work] 582}}; ὕμνους, of the nightingale, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 211 (Lyric poetry); φθόγγους ἀλύρους 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 162.7}}:—_passive_, ἅλις μοι τεθρήνηται γόοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1401; ἱκανῶς τεθρήνηται 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 20.}}
**2** c._accusative_, **bewail**, θ. πόνους {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 615; τὸν θάνατον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85a; ὅσα τὸν.. ἐμὸν θρηνῶ πατέρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 94 (_anapaest meter_), compare 530, NT.Luke.23.27, etc.; τὸν φύντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 449}}:—so also _middle_, ἄκος γὰρ οὐδὲν τόνδε θρηνεῖσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 43}}:—_passive_, **to be lamented**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 852, “fragment” 653.}}

2355:
** θρῆνος**, ὁ,
(θρέομαι) **dirge, lament**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.721, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 136, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).64, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.79, 85, etc.}}; θ. οὑμός **for** me, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 390; εἰπεῖν.. θ. θέλω ἐμὸν τὸν αὐτῆς [prev. author] “Ag.” 1322.}}
**2** **complaint, sad strain,** {{Refs|hymnus ad Panem 18}}; Τοργόνων οὔλιον θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 12.8; θρῆνοι καὶ ὀδυρμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398d, etc.}}: _plural_, **lamentations**, θρήνων ᾠδάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 88 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; title of poems by Pindar, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.39.6, etc. (Distd. from ἐπικήδειον by 1st c.BC: Trypho Grammaticus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.54 V. (cf. Ptol.Asc. p.404H.), ἐπικήδειον τὸ ἐπὶ τῷ κήδει, θ. δὲ ἐν ᾡδήποτε χρόνῳ.)}}

2356:
** θρησκ-εία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θρησκ-είη**, ἡ, (θρησκεύω) **religious worship, cult, ritual**, ἡ περὶ τὰ ἱρὰ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.18, “IG” 12(5).141.5 (Paros, 3rd c.BC), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.9.3, etc.; τοῦ Ἀπόλλωνος “SIG” 801 D (Delph., 1st c.AD); ἡ περί τινος θ. [prev. work] 867.48 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD)}}: _plural_, **rites**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.63, “PGnom.” 185 (2nd c.AD), Wilcken “Chr.” 72 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **religion, service of God**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Wis.14.18, NT.Act.26.5, NT.Jam.1.26; θ. τοῦ θεοῦ μία ἐστί, μὴ εἶναι κακόν “Corpus Hermeticum” 12 near the end}}; ἑκατέρα θ., i.e. Christianity and Paganism, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 5.69c}}; θ. τῶν ἀγγέλων **worshipping** of angels, {{NT|NT.Col.2.18.}}
**3** in bad sense, **religious formalism**, ἀντὶ ὁσιότητος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.195}}; θ. βιωτική vulgar **superstition**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4.}}

2358:
** θρῐαμβ-εύω**,
_perfect_ τεθριάμβευκα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author = 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus] “Ant.” 34}}: (θρίαμβος):—**triumph**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.7, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 1 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 45, etc.}}; ἀπό τινος **triumph over**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Rom.” 25, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Κελτική” 1; κατὰ τῆς πατρίδος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 35, compare 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.80; ἐπί τινι [prev. work] 4.31}}; also θ. ἐπὶ νίκῃ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.9.1; ἀπὸ μάχης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 9: with _accusative_ _cognate_, θ. νίκην ἄδακρυν [prev. author] 2.318b; δεύτερον θρίαμβον [prev. author] “Fab.” 23.}}
**II** **lead in triumph**, of conquered enemies, τινα {{NT|[prev. author] “Comp. Thes.Rom.” 4, NT.Col.2.15}}:—_passive_, -εύεσθαι ὑπό τινος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 35; μηδ᾽ ἐν ἐμοὶ περιίδῃς -εύομενον σεαυτόν [prev. author] “Ant.” 84.}}
**II.2** **lead in triumph**, as a general does his army, _metaphorically_, ἡμᾶς ἐν Χριστῷ {{NT|NT.2Cor.2.14.}}
**III** **divulge, noise abroad**, [{{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {ἐξεφοίτα}.}}]

2359:
** θρίξ**, ἡ,
_genitive_ τρῐχός, _dative_ _plural_ θριξί (τρίχεσιν [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.7.3 is _falsa lectio_ for{τρύχ-})}}]: - **hair**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _plural_, ὀρθαὶ τρίχες ἔσταν ἐνὶ.. μέλεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.359}}; mostly, **hair of the head**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.77, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.431; αἱ ἐν τῇ κεφαλῇ τρίχες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6}}; sheep's **wool**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.273, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 517}}; pig's **bristles**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.254, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.239}}; τρίχες ἄκραι οὐραῖαι, of a horse's tail, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.519}}; ἀνάστασις τῶν τριχῶν, of a lark's **crest**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.361.}}
**II** later in _singular_ collectively, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 535, “Ag.” 562, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 451}}; τριχὸς πλόκαμος, βόστρυχος, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 564 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 229; γενείον θρίξ [prev. author] “Pers.” 1056; κόμη θρίξ LXX.Num.6.6}}; Ἐπαφρόδιτον.. τὴν παιδικὴν τρίχα Ὑγίᾳ (i.e. ἀνέθηκεν) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 12(5).173 (Paros, 1st c.AD)}}; of a horse's **mane**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 475}}; of dogs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.8 (_sing._ and _plural_).}}
**II.2** **a single hair**, οὐδὲ τρίχ[α] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.10: proverbial, θρὶξ ἀνὰ μέσσον}} **only a hair's breadth** wanting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 6.2}}; ἄξιον τριχός, i.e. good for nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 614; οὐδ᾽ ἂν τριχὸς πριαίμην 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 7.18D.}}; ἐκ τριχὸς κρέμασθαι to hang by **a hair**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.1, 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 3.47; ἀπὸ τ. ἠερτῆσθαι “Anthologia Graeca” 5.229 (Paul. Sil.); ἐπὶ τριχὸς ἦν ἡ σωτηρία 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis” 6.6}}; εἰς ἱερὴν τρίχα ἐλθεῖν, i.e. to come to life's end, variant in {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.164 (Antip. Sid.), but compare “epigram Gr.” 248.13}}; μόνον οὐχὶ τῶν τ., φασί, λαμβάνεται 'saute aux yeux', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.257.}}
**III** in Medicine texts, **vein** on the right lobe of the liver, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.43 (variant{ἡ σῦριγξ}), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.104.}}]

2360:
** θροέω**,
_aorist_ ἐθρόησα, _poetry_ θρο- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.9, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 947, (δι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46:}} — _middle_ and _passive_ (see. below): (θρόος):—**cry aloud**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [same place], 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1410}}; παρὰ νοῦν θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1195 (Lyric poetry)}}; πᾶσιν to all, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 67, compare “Tr.” 531}}; **speak, say**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 608 (Lyric poetry)}}; θρόει, τίς..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 187 (Lyric poetry): with _accusative_ _cognate_, θ. αὐδάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 829 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1248 (Lyric poetry); λόγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1287 (Lyric poetry); πολλά [prev. author] “Aj.” 592}}; εὔφημα, ψευδῆ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 143 (Lyric poetry), 1345 (troch.)}}:—_middle_, τοῦτ᾽ ἔπος -ούμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 510 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** with _accusative_, **tell out, utter aloud**, τοὐμὸν πάθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1137 (Lyric poetry); νόμον ἄνομον [prev. work] 1141 (Lyric poetry), compare 104; πᾶς τοῦτό γ᾽ Ἑλλήνων θροεῖ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 597; θάνατόν τινι θ. [prev. work] 1425.}}—Rare. except in Trag.; in late Prose, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.10, 19.1.16.}}
**II** causal, **scare, terrify**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 180, +others}}:—_passive_, **to be stirred. moved**, of joy, ἡ κοιλία μου ἐθροήθη ἐπ᾽ αὐτόν {{LXX|LXX.Cant.5.4}}; of fear, μὴ θροεῖσθε {{NT|NT.Matt.24.6, cf. NT.2Thes.2.2.}}

2361:
** θρόμβ-ος**, ὁ,
(τρέφω, τέτροφα) **lump**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.179}}; **clot** of blood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 533, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120a, etc.; χολῆς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.75}}; of milk, **curd**, αἰγῶν ἀπόρρους θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 52.8}}; θρόμβοι ἁλῶν **coarse** salt, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**b** **drop**, θρόμβοι αἵματος καταβαίνοντες.. {{NT|NT.Luke.22.44.}}
**2** **nipple,** {{Refs|PLond. 1821.42.}}
**II** θ.· ὑψηλὸς τόπος, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2362:
** θρόνος**, ὁ,
**seat, chair**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.145, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.192e, “PMasp.” 6 ii 63 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **throne, chair of state**, θ. βασιλήϊος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.3, etc.; Ζηνὸς ἐπὶ θρόνον 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.93: _metaphorically_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553c: _plural_, ἐν θρόνοις ἥμενοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 975; ἐκ τυραννίδος θρόνων τ᾽ ἄϊστον ἐκβαλεῖν [prev. author] “Pr.” 910; Διὸς θρόνοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1041, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1732}}; **king's estate** or **dignity**, σκῆπτρα καὶ θρόνους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 425, compare 448; [γῆς] κράτη τε καὶ θρόνους νέμω [prev. author] “OT” 237, compare “Ant.” 166, etc.}}; in the Prytaneum, τῷ [Ἀπόλλωνι] θ. ἐξελεῖν {{Refs|“IG” 12.78.}}
**3** **oracular seat** of Apollo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1254, 1282 (both Lyric poetry)}}; μαντικοὶ θ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 616, etc.}}
**4** **chair of a teacher**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.2, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 819, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.174 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**5** **judge's bench**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.807b, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 10.9, 13.16.}}
**6** Astrology texts,= ὕψωμα, {{Refs|“PMich.” in “Class.Phil.” 22.22(_plural_).}}
**6.b** **favourable combination of planetary positions**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 51.}}
**II** a kind of **bread**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Neanthes Historicus 1 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**III** name of a lozenge, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 3.42, 7.12.}}

2364:
** θῠγάτηρ** [ᾰ], ἡ,
_genitive_ θυγᾰτέρος _contraction_ θυγατρό; _dative_ θυγᾰτέρι, θυγατρί; _accusative_ θυγᾰτέρα _Epic dialect_ θύγατρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.13}}; _vocative_ θύγᾰτερ: _nominative_ _plural_ θυγατέρες, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry θύγατρες {{Refs 7th c.BC+|9.144, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 16}}: _genitive_ _plural_ **-τέρων** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.832.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 461c, poetry -τρῶν: _dative_ _plural_ -τράσι}} _Epic dialect_ **-τέρεσσι**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.197}}; both sets of forms are found in poetry, θυγατρός, -τρί, -τράσι are used in Prose:—**daughter**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.148, 290, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.4, etc.}}; θύγατρες ἵππων, of mules, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 7}}; θ. ταύρων, of bees, Philo Tars. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.269: _metaphorically_, Μοισᾶν θυγατέρες, of Odes, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.3}}; πλάστιγξ ἡ χαλκοῦ θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 1.9D.}}; θ. Σειληνοῦ, of the vine, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 25}}; ψήφου συμβολικῆς θ., of a λάγυνος, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.248 (Marc. argument)}}; of villages dependent on a city, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.1.27, LXX.1Mac.5.8.}}
**II** later, **maidservant, slave**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 142.3}}. [ῡ in _Epic dialect_ in the longer forms, for the sake of meter] (Cf. Sanskrit _duhitár-_, Engl. **daughter**, etc.)

2368:
** θῡμί-ᾱμα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θῡμί-ημα**, ατος, τό, **incense**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.198, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 27, “PTeb.” 112.22 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.37J.}}; name of a particular kind (perhaps = ἀμμωνιακόν), {{Refs|“Edict.Diocl”, in Ἀθηνᾶ 18.6 (from Tegea)}}: usually in _plural_, **fragrant stuffs** for burning, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.130, 7.54, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1716, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373c, “IG” 5(2).514 (from Lycosura), NT.Rev.5.8}}; -ιάματα ἑρπετῶν **fumigations**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 6 at Title Page}}
**2** **stuff for embalming**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86, 4.71.}}

2369:
** θῡμι-ᾱτήριον**,
_Ionic dialect_ **θυμιητ-**, τό, **censer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.162, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.29, “POxy.” 521.19 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **vessel for fumigation**, {{Refs|Aë. 9.41.}}
**II** name of the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Ara”, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.11.6, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 28, etc.}}

2370:
** θῡμι-άω**,
_Ionic dialectaorist_ ἐθυμίησα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 92, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97}}:—_middle_, _Ionic dialectfuture_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.126:}}_aorist_ ἐθυμιησάμην{{Refs|[prev. work] 146, “Nat.Mul.” 7 (but -ασάμην “Morb.” 2.27)}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ᾱθήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.68.6:}}_aorist_ ἐθυμιάθην{{Refs|[prev. work] 5}}:—**burn so as to produce smoke**, θ. τὴν στύρακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.107; λιβανωτοῦ τριηκόσια τάλαντα [prev. author] 6.97; θυμιήματα [prev. author] 8.99; λιβάνου δάκρυα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 122.4}}: _absolutely_, **burn incense**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 264, LXX.2Ki.22.17, +others, “OGI” 352.37 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; τινι in honour of any one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.289f}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.51}}:—_passive_, **to be burnt**, [τὸ σπέρμα τῆς καννάβιος] θυμιᾶται (variant{-ῆται}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75; λίθος.. τεθυμιαμένος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 635}}; **pass off in fumes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362a11; θυμιωμένων τινῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 66d.}}
**2** **smoke, fumigate**, τί τινι {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2970}}:—_middle_, Hp.[same places]:—_passive_, θυμιώμεναι μέλισσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623b20.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **smoke**, ἄνθρακες θυμιῶντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 75.}}

2371:
** θῡμο-μᾰχέω**,
**to be angry**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.40.4; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] 27.8.4; πρός τινας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 22}}; τισι {{NT|NT.Act.12.20.}}
**II** **fight desperately**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.33}}; **contend obstinately**, τινι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.11.}}

2372:
** θῡμός**, ὁ,
**soul, spirit**, as **the principle of life, feeling and thought**, especially **of strong feeling and passion** (rightly derived from θύω (B) by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 419e ἀπὸ τῆς θύσεως καὶ ζέσεως τῆς ψυχῆς):}}
**I** in physical sense, **breath, life**, θ. ἀπηύρα, ἀφελέσθαι, ἐξαίνυσθαι, ὀλέσαι, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.17, 5.852, 155, 1.205: with double _accusative_, ἄμφω θ. ἀπηύρα 6.17; ἐπεί κε.. ῥεθέων ἐκ θ. ἕληται 22.68; λίπε δ᾽ ὀστέα θ. 12.386}}; ἀπὸ δ᾽ ἔπτατο θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.163; ὀλίγος δ᾽ ἔτι θ. ἐνῆεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.593; μόγις δ᾽ ἐσαγείρετο θυμόν 21.417; ἄψορρόν οἱ θ. ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ἀγέρθη 4.152; θυμοῦ καὶ ψυχῆς κεκαδών 11.334}}; of animals, {{Refs|3.294, 12.150}}, etc.: less frequently in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1388, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 620 (troch.).}}
**I.2** **spirit, strength**, τείρετο δ᾽ ἀνδρῶν θ. ὑπ᾽ εἰρεσίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.78; ἐν δέ τε θ. τείρεθ᾽ ὁμοῦ καμάτῳ τε καὶ ἱδρῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.744.}}
**I.3** πάτασσε δὲ θ. ἑκάστου each man's **heart** beat high, {{Refs|23.370, compare 7.216.}}
**II** **soul**, as shown by the feelings and passions; and so,
**II.1** **desire** or **inclination**, especially desire for meat and drink, **appetite**, πιέειν ὅτε θ. ἀνώγοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.263; πλησάμενος.. θυμὸν ἐδητύος ἠδὲ ποτῆτος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.603: generally, τά με θ. ἐνὶ στήθεσσι κελεύει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.68}}; βαλέειν δέ ἑ ἵετο θ{{Refs|. 8.301; αἲ γάρ με μένος καὶ θ. ἀνείη 22.346}}; θ. ἐποτρύνῃ [τινά] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.139}}; θ. ἐπέσσυταί τινι, ἐφορμᾶται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.173, 13.73}}; ἤθελε θυμῷ he wished **in his heart** or **with all his heart**, {{Refs|16.255, 21.65; ἵετο θυμῷ 2.589}}; so later θυμῷ βουλόμενοι wishing **with all their heart**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49; [ὄσσα ϝ] οι θ. κε θέλῃ γένεσθαι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 1.3; θυμὸς ὥρμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.25, compare 38; θυμὸς ἡδονὴν φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 286; ὧν ἐρᾷ θυμός 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.61; τῶν σφι θ. ἦν μάλιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1; ἄλλως σφι θ. ἐγένετο θεήσασθαι τὸν πόλεμον [prev. author] 8.116, etc.: with Verb omitted, σὲ γάρ μοι θῦμος ὔμνην 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 5}}; ἄρχ᾽ αὐτὸς ὥς σοι θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1319}}; ὅπου ὑμῖν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.37; βῆξαι θυμός ἐγγίνεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 8.}}
**II.2** **mind, temper, will**, θ. πρόφρων, ἵλαος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.39, 9.639; θ. ὑπερφίαλος καὶ ἀπηνής, νηλέα θ. ἔχοντας, σιδήρεος θ., 15.94, 19.229, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.191}}; ἕνα θ. ἔχειν to be of one **mind**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.710, etc.; οὐδὲ λύκοι τε καὶ ἄρνες ὁμόφρονα θ. ἔχουσιν 22.263; ἕτερος δέ με θ. ἔρυκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.302}}; ἐμὸν θ. ἔπειθεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 33; θωπείας κολακικάς, αἳ.. τοὺς θ. ποιοῦσιν κηρίνους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 633d.}}
**II.3** **spirit, courage**, μένος καὶ θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.174; θ. ἐνὶ στήθεσσι λαβεῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.461}}; πᾶσιν δὲ παραὶ ποσὶ κάππεσε θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.280}}; ψῦχρος ἔγεντο θ., of doves, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 16; θ. ἔχειν ἀγαθόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120; θ. οὐκ ἀπώλεσεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 26; ὁ θυμὸς εὐθὺς ἦν Ἀμυνίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 570}}; ἴωμεν ῥώμῃ καὶ θυμῷ ἐπί.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.21; φρονήματός τε καὶ θυμοῦ ἐμπίπλασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 411c: so in Philos., opposed to λόγος, ἐπιθυμία, [prev. work] 440b, +others,compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1328a7, 1327b24, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.26K., etc.}}; personified, **Passion, Emotion**, opposed to Λογισμός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.129.}}
**II.4** **the seat of anger**, χωόμενον κατὰ θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.429; νεμεσιζέσθω ἐνὶ θυμῷ 17.254; θυμὸν ἐχώσατο 16.616, etc.}}: hence, **anger, wrath**, δάμασον θυμόν {{Refs|9.496}}; εἴξας ᾧ θυμῷ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 598; θυμὸς μέγας ἐστὶ.. βασιλήων 2.196; θ. ὀξύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1193; θ. κρείσσων τῶν ἐμῶν βουλευμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1079, etc.}}; θυμῷ _falsa lectio_ for{θυμοῦ} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 718; οἱ τῷ θ. πραχθέντες φόνοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 867b}}; opposed to λογισμός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, etc.}}; ἐπανάγειν τὸν θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160; ἐκτείνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.31; καταθέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 567; δακεῖν [prev. author] “Nu.” 1369; θυμῷ χρᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137, +others; ὀργῆς καὶ θυμοῦ μεστοί 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.81 (so τὴν ὀργὴν καὶ τὸν θ., i.e. the outward manifestation of ὀ., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.90W.)}}; of horses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.2: _plural_ (not earlier than 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 718 (Lyric poetry))}}, **fits of anger, passions**, περὶ φόβων τε καὶ θυμῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 40e; οἵ τε θ. καὶ αἱ κολάσεις [prev. author] “Prt.” 323e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390a11.}}
**II.5** **the heart**, as the seat of the emotions, especially joy or grief, χαῖρε, γήθησε δὲ θυμῷ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.156, 7.189; θ. ἐνὶ στήθεσσι γεγήθει 13.494; μιν ἄχος κραδίην καὶ θ. ἵκανεν 2.171; ἄχνυτο θ. 14.39, etc.}}; δόκησε δ᾽ ἄρα σφίσι θ. ὣς ἔμεν ὡς εἰ.. they felt as glad at **heart** as if.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.415}}; μηδ᾽ ὀνίαισι δάμνα.. θ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.4}}; of fear, δέος ἔμπεσε θυμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.625, compare 8.138}}; of love, τὴν ἐκ θυμοῦ φίλεον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|9.343; ἐκ θυμοῦ στέργοισα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.130}}; ἐμῷ κεχαρισμένε θυμῷ my **heart's** beloved, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.243}}; reversely, ἀπὸ θ. μᾶλλον ἐμοὶ ἔσεαι wilt be alien from my **heart**, {{Refs|1.562}}; ἐκ θ. πεσέειν, i.e. to lose thy favour,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.595; ἔρωτι θυμὸν ἐκπλαγεῖσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 8; ἐκ θ. κλαῦσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas 11.}}
**II.6** **mind, soul, as the seat of thought**, ταῦθ᾽ ὥρμαινε κατὰ φρένα καὶ κατὰ θ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.193, etc.; ᾔδεε γὰρ κατὰ θ. 2.409, compare 4.163, etc.}}; φράζετο θυμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.646; ἐν θ. ἐβάλοντο ἔπος 15.566; τοὺς λόγους θυμῷ βάλε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 706; εἰς θ. βαλεῖν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 975}}; οὐκ ἐς θ. φέρω I bring him not into my **mind** or **thoughts**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1347.}}

2373:
** θῡμόω**,
**make angry, provoke**, {{LXX|LXX.Hos.12.14(15)}}: once in Trag., ὥστε θυμῶσαι φρένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 581.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, _2nd pers._ _singular_ θυμοῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 584}}: _future_ **-ώσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1069}}, **-ωθήσομαι** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Job.21.4, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.89 W}}.: _aorist_ ἐθυμωσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1343 (Lyric poetry), more frequently ἐθυμώθην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, +others}}; _participle_ **-θείς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 461, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 931b}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ τεθυμῶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 478, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 346a}}:—**to be wroth** or **angry**, _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” [same place], 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88, etc.; θυμοῦ δι᾽ ὀργῆς ἥτις ἀγριωτάτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 344}}; εἰς ἔριν θ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1018}}; of animals, **to be wild, restive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 477, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.10}}; θυμοῦσθαι εἰς κέρας **vent fury** with the horns, Virgil's **irasci in cornua**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 743; compare ἀοιδὸς ἐς κέρας τεθύμωται 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.321}}; τὸ θυμούμενον **passion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.68}}; θυμοῦσθαί τινι **to be angry with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 733, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 543, 1230, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324a; ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52; περί τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1368 (probably to be read instead of μυθοῦσθαι); βοῦς πρὸς τὸν ἐλαύνοντα -ωθείς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 38; σοι θυγατέρος -ούμενος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 751 (troch.): with _dative_ _of things_, τῇ ξυντυχίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1006.}}

2374:
** θύρα** [ῠ],
_Ionic dialect_ **θύρη**, ἡ, _Ionic dialect_ _genitive_ _plural_ θυρέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 127, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9}}:— **door**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.317, etc.}}: frequently in _plural_ of **double** or **folding doors**, θ. δικλίδες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.267; θ. φαειναί 6.19, +others; θυρῶν ζεῦγος καινῶν “IG” 12.313.123, compare 4.1488.25(Epid.)}}; ἡ δεξιὰ θ. the right **valve**, {{Refs|[prev. work]22.1457.16}}; θ. μονόθυρος {{Refs|[prev. work]1627.418}}; θύραι λίθιναι (including the framework) {{Refs|[prev. work] 12.372.195}}; θύραι αὔλειαι, see at {αὔλειο; ἡ θ. ἡ εἰς τὸν κῆπον φέρονσα} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.53, compare κηπαῖος 11}}; rarely for πύλαι, **gates**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 65}}; of the **carceres** in the Roman circus, **barriers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Tab.Defix.Aud. 187.59. —Phrases: προσθεῖναι τὰς θ., προστιθέναι τὴν θ., 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.13; ἐπισπάσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.36; κλείειν 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314d; ἐφέλκεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 16}}; τὴν θ. βαλανοῦν, μοχλοῦν, bar **the door**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 251, 369}}; θύραν κόπτειν, πατάσσειν, κρούειν, knock, rap at **the door**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 132, “Ra.” 38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310b}}; ἀράττειν, ἐπαράξαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 977, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314d}}; τὴν θ. ἀνοιγνύναι open it, see at {ἀνοίγνυμ; ὦσαι} push **it** open, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.24}}; μικρὸν ἐνδοῦναι open **it** a little, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.597d; δόμου ἐν πρώτῃσι θύρῃσι στῆναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.255; ἷζε δ᾽ ἐπὶ.. οὐδοῦ ἔντοσθε θυράων 17.339; θυρῶν ἔνδον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 78; πρὸ θυρῶν [prev. work] 109 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἐπί or παρὰ Πριάμοιο θύρῃσι at Priam's **door**, i.e. before his dwelling, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.788, 7.346: _metaphorically_, ἐπὶ ταῖς θύραις τῆς Ἑλλάδος εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.23, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.34; τῆς πατρίδος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 29, “Arat.” 37; ἐπὶ θύραις τῆς Πίσης 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.15}}; πυρετοῦ περὶ θύρας ὄντος being **at the door**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.128f (but χειμῶνος ἐπὶ θύραις ὄντος 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum librum primum commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 130.25).}}
**2** especially of kings and potentates, οἱ τῶν ἀρίστων Περσῶν παῖδες ἐπὶ ταῖς βασιλέως θύραις παιδεύονται are educated at **court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.3}}; γυνὴ φοιτῶσα ἐπὶ τὰς θύρας τοῦ βασιλέος, of a petitioner, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.8}}; αἱ ἐπὶ τὰς θ. φοιτήσεις dangling after **the court**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 1.6.7; ἐπὶ ταῖς τῶν πλουσίων θ. διατρίβειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1391a12; περὶ θύρας διατρίβειν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1313b7, 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 121}}; applied also to lovers, clients, disciples, etc., ἐπὶ τὴν θύραν (or τὰς θύρας) τινὸς βαδίζειν, ἰέναι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1007, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364b, compare “Phdr.” 233e, etc.; ἐπὶ ταῖσι θύραις ἀεὶ καθῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 467: _metaphorically_, Μουσῶν ἐπὶ ποιητικὰς θ. ἀφικέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245a.}}
**3** proverbial, γλώσσῃ θύραι οὐκ ἐπίκεινται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 421}}; οὐδέποτ᾽ ἴσχει θ., of admirers of the Demos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 265}}; ἐπὶ θύραις τὴν ὑδρίαν to break the pitcher **at the** very **door**, 'there's many a slip 'twixt cup and lip', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1363a7}}; τίς ἂν θύρας ἁμάρτο; {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 993b5; λόγος δικαστηρίου ἢ ἀγορᾶς οὐδὲ θύρας ἰδών 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 23}}; τὸ κατὰ θύραν τερπνόν **vulgar** pleasures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.496 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; παρὰ θύραν πλανᾶσθαι 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.43}}; ἐκ θυρῶν εὐθέως τῆς.. ἀκροάσεως at the very **beginning**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 2.1.}}
**4** **shutter** of a window, τὰς θ. τὰς ἐπὶ τῶν θυρίδων {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).872.37 (from Tenos), compare 22.1668.60.}}
**5** _plural_, **door** of a chariot, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.9.}}
**6** _plural_, **axle-trees**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.146 (variant{εὑραί}).}}]
**7** θύρη καταπακτή trap-**door**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16.}}
**8** **frame of planks, raft**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.96}}; φραξάμενοι τὴν ἀκρόπολιν θύρῃσί τε καὶ ξύλοισι with **hurdles** and logs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.51, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101.}}
**9** in war, **fenced works** to obstruct landingparties, in _plural_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 94.37, 100.7.}}
**II** generally, **entrance**, as to a grotto, in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.243, +others}}
**II.2** **sluice-gate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.134: _plural_, [prev. work]2p.41 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** _metaphorically_, senses, as **the entrances to the soul**, τὸ σῶμα πολλαῖς θυρίσι καὶ θύραις ἀνοίγοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Serenus Geometra cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.6.17; ἐγγὺς τοῦ στόματος ἡ καρδία, ἡ δὲ ψυχὴ τῶν θ. 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.7. (I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **dhur-**, cf. Latin _foras_, **fores**, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **duru** 'door', etc.)

2375:
** θῠρεός**, ὁ,
(θύρα) **stone put against a door** to keep it shut, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.240, 313.}}
**II** **oblong shield (shaped like a door)**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.428.36 (3rd c.BC)}}, Inscription cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 26, 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2}}; hence, of the Roman **scutum** (opposed to ἀσπίς,= **clipeus**), {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.30.3, 6.23.2, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.16, cf. NT.Eph.6.16, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 163.2, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 3.2, etc.}}
**III** **disk** forming part of καθετήρ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).287 B 68 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** in Mathematics texts, **oval**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” 1 “Deff.” 3,8.}}

2376:
** θῠρίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
_diminutive_ of θύρα, **window**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Praxilla Lyrica 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 379, “Th.” 797, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404a4, “Ath.” 50.2, “IG” 11(2).161 D 101 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1116.23 (1st c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.273b}}; **window-frame**, ἐναρμόσαι εἰς ἑκάστην τὴν θ. (**opening**) χαλκᾶς θ. (**frames**) {{Refs|“IG” 22.1668.37.}}
**b** **audience-window** of the king or high officials in Egypt, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“UPZ” 15.7, 16.20, 53.5 (2nd c.BC), 4th c.BC: Heraclides Cumaeus Historicus 4.}}
**2** **opening** at each end of a bee's cell, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624a7.}}
**3** **valve** of a bivalve fish, {{Refs|[prev. work] 529b7.}}
**4** in _plural_, **embrasures** in battlements, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 22.463.55, +others; for artillery, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.91, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.10.}}
**II** in _plural_, **planks, boards**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus Pont. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.521f}}; **tablets**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.2** **cell** of wasps, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 628a20, 629a30.}}

2378:
** θῠσί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, (θύω) properly **burnt-offering, sacrifice**, mostly in _plural_, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 176, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 128.6, etc.; ἐν θυσίησι εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.99; θυσίαισι δέκεσθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.86, compare “I.” 5(4).30; θυσίῃσι ἱλάσκεσθαι τὸν θεόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, compare 6.105}}; θυσίας ἔρδειν, ἐπιτελέειν, ἀνάγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.131, 2.63, 60: also in _sing._, θυσίαν ποιεῖσθαι, θύειν, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174c, “R.” 362c; ἄγειν [prev. author] Alcaeus2.148e}}; θ. σωτηρίου, αἰνέσεως, τῆς τελειώσεως, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.3.1, 7.2, LXX.Exo.29.34}}; of the gods, θυσίαν δέχεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 701 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** **mode of sacrifice**, θ. ἡ αὐτὴ πᾶσι κατέστηκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.60, compare 2.39.}}
**3** **festival, at which sacrifices were offered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, “Ti.” 26e, +others; θ. καὶ διαγωγαὶ τοῦ συζῆν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b37, compare “EN” 1160a20}}; περὶ τῶν ἐν Ῥόδῳ θ., title of work by Theognis Hist.
**3.b** generally, **rite, ceremony**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.693f, “Thes.” 20.}}
**II** **victim, offering**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.184f, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 12.}}

2380:
** θύω**,
**A**_imperfect_ ἔθυον, _Epic dialect_ θῦον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.222}}, _Ionic dialect_ θύεσκον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 37:}}_future_ θύσω [ῡ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1141, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909d, 4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 5.10}}, _Doric dialect_ θυσῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.33}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ θυσέοντι {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).452 (from Thera)}}: _aorist_ ἔθῡσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.231, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ θῦσα {{Refs|14.446}}: _perfect_ τέθῠκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1062, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328c}}:—_middle_, _future_ θύσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 340}} (as _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.197)}}: _aorist_ ἐθυσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92, (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.91, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ τῠθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.91, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 4.2:}}_aorist_ ἐτύθην [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.216, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 242, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 155.2}} (_participle_ written θυθέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 185, compare τὴν βοῦν τὴν θυθεῖσαν “IG” 12(7).241 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC), etc.)}}: _perfect_ τέθῠμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 341 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1034, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.5 (in middle sense, 5.1.18, “An.” 7.8.21)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐτέθῠτο {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.1.23}}. [ῡ in _future_ and _aorist_, ῠ in _perfect_ _active_ and _passive_, and _aorist_ _passive_; ῡ generally in _present_ and _imperfect_, except in _trisyllable_ cases of _participle_, θῠοντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.260, θύ?~Xοντες “hymnus ad Apollinem” 491, but θύ?~Xεσκε 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 37}}; ἔθύ?~Xε, θύ?~Xων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).57, 13.69}}; θύ?~Xειν, at the end of a line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1141 (assuming variant, perhaps θύη), “Cyc.” 334, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 792 (spoken by a Megarian)}}; θύ?~Xεις, θύ?~Xω, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Strato Comicus 1.19, 20}}; θύ?~Xωντι _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _present_ _subjunctive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 4.21.]}}
**A.I** _active_, **offer by burning** meat or drink to the gods (τὸ θύειν δωρεῖσθαί ἐστι τοῖς θεοῖς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 14c), θεοῖσι δὲ θῦσαι ἀνώγει Πάτροκλον.., ὁ δ᾽ ἐν πυρὶ βάλλε θυηλάς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.219, compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in Scholia (at [prev. work]), “Comica Adespota” 7D. (cited in 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.74 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus)}}; ἦ ῥα καὶ ἄρ γματα θῦσε θεοῖς, of a drink-offering, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.446, compare 15.260}}; so ἔνθα δὲ πῦρ κήαντες ἐθύσαμεν (i.e. τῶν τυρῶν) **made an offering** of cheese,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.231; θ. ἀκρόθινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).57}}; πέλανον, δεῖπνα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 204, “Eu.” 109}}; πυρούς, ναστούς, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 565, 567: with _dative_ _of things_, θ. τούτῳ ὅ τι ἔχοι ἕκαστος (with variant{τοῦτο}) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50.}}]
**A.I.2** **sacrifice, slay** a victim, [τῷ ἡλίῳ] θ. ἵππους (variant{ἵπποισι}){{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 216; ταῦρον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.69; αὑτοῦ παῖδα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1417, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 532, etc.; ἱρά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; ἱερεῖα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; θ. θῦμα, θυσίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 290e, “R.” 362c, etc.}}; θ. διαβατήρια, ἐπινίκια, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173a, etc.:}} —_passive_, τὰ τεθυμένα **the flesh of the victim**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.14, etc.}}; τὰ τεθ. ἱερά{{Refs|[prev. work] 3.5.5; τὰ θυόμενα [prev. author] “Lac.” 15.3.}}
**A.I.2.b** simply, **slaughter**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1062, LXX.Isa.22.13.}}
**A.I.3** _absolutely_, **offer sacrifice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31. +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 594, “fragment” 161.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1159}}; τοῖσι θεοῖσι θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 23, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.60, 8.138; θεῶν ἕνεκα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 129.1.}}
**A.I.4** **celebrate** with offerings or sacrifices, σῶστρα θ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.118; γενέθλια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.121c; Λύκαια, Ἡράκλεια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.10, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.86; ἐλευθέρια 4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 5.10; γάμους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 55.}}
**A.I.5** with _doubleaccusative_, εὐαγγέλια θ. ἑκατὸν βοῦς **sacrifice** a hundred oxen **for** the good news, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 656.}}
**A.I.6** Ἑστίᾳ θύειν, proverbial of niggards, because sacrifices to Hestia admitted no one to share the offering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 28.}}
**A.II** _middle_, **cause** a victim **to be offered**, τῶν θυμάτων ὧν δεῖ θύεσθαι καὶ παρίστασθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.65 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.: hence frequently _absolutely_, **consult the gods**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.189, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 340}}; ἐπὶ Κρότωνα, ἐπὶ τῷ Πέρσῃ, i.e. on marching **against.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.44, 9.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.21; θύεσθαι ἐπ᾽ ἐξόδῳ [prev. work] 6.4.9; ὑπὲρ τῆς μονῆς [prev. work] 5.6.27: with}}_infinitive_, θ. ἰέναι **offer sacrifice** [to learn] whether to go or not, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.2.3; also ἐθυόμην εἰ βέλτιον εἴη [prev. work] 6.1.31 (so in}}_active_, ἔθυε (variant{ἐθύετο}) τῷ Διί.. πότερά οἱ λῷον καὶ ἄμεινον εἴη.. {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.6.44)}}; διαβατήρια θύεσθαι, as in _active_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.54.}}
**A.II.2** _metaphorically_, **tear in pieces**, of wild beasts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 137 (Lyric poetry). (Hence θυμός}}, cf. Sanskrit_dhūmás_, Latin _fumus_ 'smoke', θυμιάω, θύος, θυήλημα, τύφω, perhaps θεῖον (A), Latin _suffire_; compare {θύω})

2382:
** θώραξ**, ᾱκος,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **θώρηξ**, ηκος, _Aeolic dialect_ **θόρραξ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15 (codices 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus)}}, ὁ:— **corslet**, θ. χάλκεος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.560; παναίολος 11.374; πολυδαίδαλος 4.136, compare 11.19, etc.; δεκάμνουν θώρηκος κύτος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1224; ἔξαιρε παῖ θώρακα.. τὸν χοᾶ [prev. author] “Ach.” 1133; θ... γυάλοισιν ἀρηρώς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.529 (γύαλα explained as front- and back-piece fastened with περόναι, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.26.5); θώρηκος γύαλον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.99}}; ὅθι διπλόος ἤντετο θ{{Refs|. 4.133; κατὰ ζώνην θώρηκος ἔνερθε 11.234}}; linen **jerkin** (not worn by Homeric Greeks _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.529, but compare λινοθώρηξ), θόρρακες νέω λίνω 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.182, 3.47, “Chron.Lind.” C. 36, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.19.7.}}
**2** **coat of mail, scale armour**, θ. χρύσεος λεπιδωτός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.22, compare 74; φολιδωτός 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 26.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.21.6}}; of chain mail, see at {ἁλυσιδωτός}.
**2.b** **slough** of a serpent, καθάπερ ὄφις παλαιὸν ἀποδύεται θ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 88.}}
**II** **part covered by the** θώραξ {{Refs|1}}, **trunk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1095, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a5; κεφαλῆς καὶ θώρακος καὶ τῆς κάτω κοιλίας [prev. author] “Pr.” 962a34}}; sometimes taken as extending below the midriff, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69e; ἀπ᾽ αὐχένος μέχρι αἰδοίων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491a30, compare “PA” 686b5, ἐν τῷ κάτω θώρακος χωρίῳ}}, of the abdominal cavity, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.448}}; but also of the **chest, thorax**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a17}}:—there is a play on _significations_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1 and 11 in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1194f}}
**II.b** **thorax** of crustaceans, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 601a13, +others}}
**II.2** **bandage for the chest**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.48 at Title Page, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 33, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).817.}}
**III** ={θωράκιον} 11, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.10.}}

2386:
** ἴᾱμα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἴημα**, ατος, τό, (ἰάομαι) **remedy, medicine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771c, etc.}}; στεναγμοί, τῶν πόνων ἰάματα variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 385.}}
**II** ={ἴασις}, ἰάματα τοῦ Ἀπόλλωνος καὶ τοῦ Ἀσκλαπιοῦ {{NT|“IG” 4.951.2(Epid.), cf. NT.1Cor.12.9 (_plural_).}}
**II.2** **soothing, pacification**, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.10.4.}}

2390:
** ἰάομαι**,
_imperative_ ἰῶ (see. below), _Ionic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἰᾶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 24 (ἰῆσθαι variant in [prev. author] “Morb.Sacr.” 13), 5th c.AD: Cypria ἰ}}**j**ᾶσθαι {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.3 H}}.: _future_ ἰάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1107, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.69}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ἰήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.525, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 13, (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.6}}: _aorist_ ἰασάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1072, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἰησάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.899, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 2:}} —_passive_ (see. below). [ῑ- in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; also ῐ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 597]}}:—**heal, cure**, in _present_ and _imperfect_, **attempt to cure, treat**, of persons or bodies, etc., τινα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.2, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, etc.; τοὺς κάμνοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 299a, compare 293b; ὀφθαλμόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.525; τὸ σῶμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1210: _absolutely_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.520, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.899: proverbial, ὁ τρώσας ἰάσεται “Mantissa Proverbiorum” 2.28.}}
**2** **cure. treat**, of diseases, νόσους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.46, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 597, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 340e, “Chrm.” 156b, etc.; σμύρνῃσι ἰ. τὰ ἕλκεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.181}}: _metaphorically_, **remedy**, δύσγνοιαν, ἀδικίαν ἰᾶσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1107, “Or.” 650; ἀτυχίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.101; δωροδόκημα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.69; ἀσάφειαν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 1.3: proverbial, μὴ τῷ κακῷ τὸ κακὸν ἰῶ}}, i.e. do not make bad worse, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65; μὴ κακοῖς ἰῶ κακά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 349; κακοῖς ὅταν θέλωσιν ἰᾶσθαι κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 77: _absolutely_, οὔτε τι γὰρ κλαίων ἰήσομαι 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 13.}}
**3** **cure the effects of, counteract**, ἄκρατος ἰ. τὸ κώνειον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.653a.}}
**4** **repair**, τὸ βλαβέν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933e; τὴν φύσιν τὴν ἀνθρωπίνην [prev. author] “Smp.” 191d; θυσιαστήριον LXX.1Ki.18.32; δίκελλαν 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 27.3.}}
**II** _active_ only _aorist 1_ ἰάσαμεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.453}}; _participle_ ἰάσαντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1236:}}_aorist_ ἰάθην is always _passive_, **be healed, recover**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.9, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.330 (4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator), “IG” 4.951.113(Epid.), etc.; ἀπὸ τῶν νόσων NT.Luke.6.17}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἰήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.3, “Int.” 1}}: _future_ ἰαθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 14, “Geoponica” 12.25.3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.377; ἰάσομαι 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.317 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: _perfect_ ἴᾱμαι {{NT|NT.Mark.5.29.}}

2392:
** ἴᾱσις** [ῑ],
_Ionic dialect_ **ἴησις**, εως, ἡ, (ἰάομαι) **healing, mode of healing, remedy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.17, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 68, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188c; οἷς [πήμασιν] ἴ. οὐκ ἔνεστ᾽ ἰδεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 876}}; [ἀδίκημα] οὗ μή ἐστιν ἴ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1374b31, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.94, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.29.2; ἔλεγχος ἰ. τοῦ λόγου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1009a21}}: _plural_, **cures**, ἰάσεις ἀποτελῶ {{NT|NT.Luke.13.32.}}
**2** **mending, repairs**, ζυγάστρου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 244i53 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**3** Alch., **cupellation, refining,** {{Refs|PLeid.X. 21.}}

2393:
** ἴασπ-ῐς**, ιδος
(but _accusative_ ἴασπιν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 267, 613)}}, ἡ, **jasper**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110d, “IG” 22.1388.88, 7.2420 (Thebes, 3rd c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 23, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.746 (from Polemo).}}
**II** ={χρυσόγονον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.56}}. (Cf. Hebrew **yāšpheh**).

2395:
** ἰᾱτρός**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἰητρός**, ὁ, (ἰάομαι) like{ἰατήρ}, **one who heals, physician** or **surgeon**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.28, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130s q.; ἰητρὸς ἀνήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.514}}; φὼς ἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 261}}; ἥρως ἰ., worshipped at Athens and elsewhere, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.249, “IG” 22.840, “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 263, etc.}}; οὐ πρὸς ἰατροῦ σοφοῦ θρηνεῖν ἐπῳδὰς πρὸς τομῶντι πήματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 581}}; ἰατρῶν παῖδες, for ἰατροί, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist. Conscr.” 7}}; as a name of Apollo, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 584 (_anapaest meter_), 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1207, “IPE” 2.6 (from Panticapaeum); ἰ. ὀφθαλμῶν, κεφαλῆς, ὀδόντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.84: as _feminine_, of Artemis, 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus}} Trag.1.5; of Aphrodite, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.143d: _plural_, of certain Nymphs in Elis, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; **midwife**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Helladius Epigrammaticus cited in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. “Bibliotheca - Patrologia Graeca” p.531 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {μαῖα}.}}]
**II** _metaphorically_, εὐφροσύνα πόνων ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.2}}; ὦ θάνατε,.. τῶν ἀνηκέστων κακῶν ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 255; ὁ θάνατος λοῖσθος ἰ. νόσων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 698; ὀργῆς νοσούσης εἰσὶν ἰατροὶ λόγοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 380, compare “Ch.” 699}}; [ἀτυχίας] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.13; τῆς πόλεως [κακῶς] βουλευσαμένης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.14; λύπης ἰ. χρόνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 117; τῆς ὕβρεως 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.627e: Comically, βουλιμίας, of a table, 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 13.3}}; γῆς ἰ., of a farmer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 16. [ῑᾱ Trag., also 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 259, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 88, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 497, etc.: ῐα in [5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus] 157, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1072, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 363, “Pl.” 406, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 282, etc.: ῑᾱ monosyllable, “TAM” 2(1).369.]}}

2397:
** ἰδέα** [ῐ],
_Ionic dialect_ **ἰδέη**, ἡ, (ἰδεῖν) **form**, ἰδέᾳ καλός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).103, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.6; τὴν ἰ. πάνυ καλός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315e; τὴν ἰ. μοχθηρός 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.100, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1000; ἰδέην ὁρέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80}}; opposed to χρῶμα, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.109}}; opposed to μέγεθος, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109b (_plural_); ἡ ἰ. αὐτοῦ ἦν ὡς ἀστραπή NT.Matt.28.3, etc.}}; of the elementary **shapes**, ἄτομοι ἰδέαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1111a codices, compare “fragment” 141 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; of the four **elements**, Philistion cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Anonymus Londinensis 20.25.}}
**2** **semblance**, opposed to reality, γνώμην ἐξαπατῶσ᾽ ἰδέαι **outward appearances** cheat the mind, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 128.}}
**3** **kind, sort**, φύλλα τοιῆσδε ἰδέης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.203}}; φύσιν παρέχονται ἰδέης τοιήνδε [οἱ ποτάμιοι ἵπποι] {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.71}}; ἐφρόνεον διφασίας ἰ. they conceived two **modes** of acting, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.100, compare 119}}; τὰ ὄργι᾽ ἐστὶ τίν᾽ ἰ. ἔχοντά σο; what is their **nature** or **fashion?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 471}}; ἑτέραν ὕμνων ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 384}}; καινὰς ἰ. εἰσφέρειν new **forms** of comedy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 547}}; τίς ἰ. βουλεύματο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 993}}; πᾶσα ἰ. θανάτου every **form** of death, or death in every **form**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.81, compare 83, 2.51; πολλαὶ ἰ. πολέμων [prev. author] 1.109; ἡ ὑπάρχουσα ἰ. τῆς παρασκευῆς [prev. author] 4.55}}; πᾶσαν ἰδέαν πειράσαντες having tried every **way**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.19}}; τῇ αὐτῇ ἰ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.62, 6.76}}; οὐκ ἐν ταῖς αὐταῖς ἰ. not in the same **relations**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.44: εἰς μίαν τινὰ ἰ}}. into one **kind of existence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 184d; ἄλλη ἰ. πολιτείας [prev. author] “R.” 544c, etc.; ἀγοραίας.. ἰδέας τοῦ βίου 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 196.}}
**4** especially in Rhet., etc., of literary **form**, ἀμφοτέραις ταῖς ἰδέαις κατεχρήσαντο πρὸς τὴν ποίησιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.48, compare 15.47,183}}; ἡ ἰαμβικὴ ἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449b8, compare 1450b34, “Rh.Al.” 1425a9, etc.}}; ἡ ἐν τῷ λέγειν ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.258 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**4.b** **style**, Πλατωνική, Δημοσθενικὴ ἰ., {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Hermogenem commentaria” 1.112 R.}}
**4.c** **a quality of style** (e.g. σαφήνεια, γοργότης, etc.), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “[prev. author]” at Title Page, etc.}}
**II** in Logic,= εἶδος, **class, kind**: hence, **principle of classification**, ἔφησθα.. μιᾷ ἰδέᾳ τά τε ἀνόσια ἀνόσια εἶναι καὶ τὰ ὅσια ὅσια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 6d, compare “Phdr.” 265d. “Sph.” 253d, etc.}}
**II.2** _plural_ in Platonic Philosophy, **ideal forms, archetypes**, τὰς.. ἰ. νοεῖσθαι μέν, ὁρᾶσθαι δ᾽ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 507b, compare 596b, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 990a34, +others, “EN” 1096a17: also in _sing._, ἡ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ ἰ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 508e, +others, compare εἶδος.}}
**II.3** **notion, idea**, προάγειν τὸν ἀποκρινόμενον ἐπὶ τὴν ἰ. ἀγνοουμένου πράγματος {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Nausiphanes Philosophus 2. (Written εἰδέα in later Greek, as “PGen.” 16.17 (3rd c.AD), variant in NT.Matt.28.3.)}}

2398:
** ἴδιος** [ῐδ], α, ον,
_Attic dialect_ also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 349b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 532b32 (see. entry near the end)}}:
**I** **one's own, pertaining to oneself**: hence,
**I.1** **private, personal** (opposed to κοινός): twice in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, πρῆξις δ᾽ ἥδ᾽ ἰδίη οὐ δήμιος}} this business is **private**, not public, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.82; δήμιον ἦ ἴδιο; 4.314}}; ἴδιος ἐν κοινῷ σταλείς embarking as **a private man** in a public cause, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.49}}; ἰ. στόλῳ χρᾶσθαι, opposed to δημοσίῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63; γῆς.. νοσούσης ἴ. κινοῦντες κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 636; κοινὸν ἐξ ἰδίας ἀνοίας κακόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 641 (Lyric poetry), compare “Or.” 766 (troch.)}}; ἴδια πράσσων ἢ στρατοῦ ταχθεὶς ὕπ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 1363 (troch.); ἴ. κέρδεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100; συμφορά 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.11; πρόσοδος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.11; τὰ ἴ. διάφορα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37; πλοῦτος ἴ. καὶ δημόσιος [prev. author] 1.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521a; ἴ. οὐ κοινὸς πόνος [prev. work] 535b, compare 543b}}; ξυμβόλαια{{Refs|[prev. work] 443e; ἰ. ἢ πολιτικὴ πρᾶξις [prev. author] “Grg.” 484d; πόλεις καὶ ἴ. οἶκοι [prev. author] “Lg.” 890b, compare 796d}}, etc.; τὰ ἱρά, opposed to τὰ ἴ., temples, opposed to **private buildings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9, 8.109}}; τὸ ἐν ἰδίοις discussion among **private persons**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 225b.}}
**I.2** **one's own**, opposed to ἀλλότριος, ἐπικώμια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.32; ἡ ἰ. ἐλευθερίη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147; Ζεὺς ἰδίοις νόμοις κρατύνων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 404 (Lyric poetry); ἰδίᾳ γνώμᾳ [prev. work] 543 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὔτοι τὰ χρήματ᾽ ἴ. κέκτηνται βροτοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 555}}; φίλων οὐδὲν ἴ., ={κοινὰ τὰ τῶν φίλων}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Andr.” 376: with _pronoun_, χωρίον ἡμέτερον ἴδιον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.8.}}
**I.3** τὰ ἴ. **private interests**, opposed to public, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82, 2.61, etc.}}; **one's own property**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.141, etc.}}; τὰ ἴ. πράττειν mind **one's own business**, in later Gr., {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 405, cf. NT.1Thes.4.11}}; μένειν ἐπὶ τῶν ἰ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.57.5}}; εἰς τὸ ἴ. καταθέσθαι for **self**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.3, etc.: with _pronoun_, τοὐμὸν ἴ. εἰπεῖν}} my **personal** opinion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.8}}; τὰ ἐμὰ ἴ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.66}}; τὰ αὑτοῦ ἴ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 440 (uncertain reading), compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.127}}; τὰ ὑμέτερα ἴ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.307}}; τὰ ἴ. σφῶν αὐτῶν, τὰ ἴ. τὰ σφέτερα αὐτῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.2, 3.36}}; ἔγωγε τοὐμὸν ἴ. I **for my own part**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 9.}}
**I.4** of persons, **personally attached** to one, ἴδιοι Σελεύκου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.6.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1315a36, “UPZ” 146.38 (2nd c.BC), 109.18 (1st c.BC)}}; ἄνθρωπος ἴδιος τῇ εὐνοίᾳ τῇ πρὸς.. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 32 (3rd c.BC)}}; ταῖς εὐνοίαις ἴδιοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.26}}; ἴδιοι, οἱ, **members of one's family, relatives**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 665 ii 1 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 70.5, etc.}}
**I.5** ἡ ἰ. (sometimes with κώμη added, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 15.13 (2nd c.AD))}}, one's **place of origin,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 327.28 (2nd c.AD), etc.: _plural_, καταπορεύεσθαι εἰς τὰς ἰ.[prev. work]5.7 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.6** in later Gr., almost as a possessive _pronoun_,= ἑαυτοῦ, ἑαυτῶν, ἡ ἰ. φιλαγαθία {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1011.71 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; χρῶνται ὡς ἰδίοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 11.14 (2nd c.BC)}}; περὶ τῶν ἰ. βιβλίων, title of work by Galen.
**I.6.b** ἴ. θάνατος **one's own**, i.e. a **natural** death, Ramsay **Cities and Bishoprics**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|No. 133; ἰδίοις τελευτῶσι θανάτοις 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 199; also ἰδία μοίρῃ Ramsay op.cit. No. 187.}}
**II** **separate, distinct**, ἔθνος ἴ. καὶ οὐδαμῶς Σκυθικόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.18, compare 22}}; ἴδιοί τινές σοι [θεοί]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 890; ἑκάστῳ τῶν ὀνομάτων ὑπόκειταί τις ἴδιος οὐσία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 349b}}; πόλεις.. βαρβάρους καὶ ἰδίας Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.183}}; ὁ βάτραχος ἰδίαν ἔχει τὴν γλῶτταν, τὴν ἰ. ἀφίησι φωνήν, a **peculiar** kind of tongue,.. its **peculiar** note, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536a8, 11: followed by ἤ, ἴδιον ἔπασχεν πάθος ἢ οἱ ἄλλοι}} **unique and different** from others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 481c; so ἴδιον παρὰ τὰ ἄλλα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.4.10.}}
**II.b** ἴ. λόγος, in Ptolemaic and Roman Egypt, **private account**, δεδώκαμεν Πύρωνι τὸν ἔσχατόν σου ἴ. λόγον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 253 (3rd c.BC),compare “PGrenf.” 1.16 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; later, **special account**, a branch of the fiscal administration, Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 162 (2nd c.BC), “PAmh.” 2.31 (2nd c.BC), “PGnom.Prooem.” (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; ὁ γνώμων τοῦ ἰ. λόγου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.44 (1st c.AD)}}; also as the title of the Controller, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.12 codices, “OGI” 408 (2nd c.AD), Mitteis “Chr.” 372 vi 1 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **strange, unusual**, ἰδίοισιν ὑμεναίοισι κοὐχὶ σώφροσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 558}}; **peculiar, exceptional**, περιττὸν καὶ ἴ. γένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 760a5; τὰ περιττὰ καὶ ἴ. τῶν δένδρων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.7.1}}; παράδοξον εἰπεῖν τι καὶ περιττὸν καὶ ἴ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1068b}}; **eccentric**, of persons, {{Refs|[prev. work]57e}}; ἴ. τις ἐν πᾶσι βουλόμενος εἶναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Them.” 18.}}
**II.3** **peculiar, appropriate**, ἴδια ὀνόματα **proper, specific** words, opposed to περιέχοντα, class-names, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407a31; ὄνομα ἴ. τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 580e; τὸ ἴ. τοῦ ἐπαίνου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 19.}}
**III** ἴ. λόγοι **ordinary private** conversation, opposed to ποίησις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366e, compare “Euthd.” 305d; see infr. VI. 2b.}}
**IV** τὸ ἴ. **characteristic property** of a species, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 102a18, 103b11, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.75, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.5.13}}; but also, **distinguishing feature** in a relative sense, ἴ. πρός τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 128b25.}}
**V** regular _comparative_ ἰδιώτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.73, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.1.6}}: _superlative_ **-ώτατος**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.65, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.14.2}}; also ἰδιαίτερος, -αίτατος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 656a26, 658b33; -αίτατος but not -αίτερος _accusative_ to Thom.Mag. p.189R.}}
**VI** _adverb_ **ἰδίως**, **peculiarly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.108}}; **severally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 807b}}: _comparative_ ἰδιωτέρως [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.13.4; ὡς -ώτερον εἰπεῖν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.68 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἰδιαίτερον 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.6.7: _superlative_ ἰδιώτατα (variant{-αίτατα}) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.1}}]; ἰδίως καλεῖσθαι to be called **specifically**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394b28; -αίτατα λέγεσθαι [prev. author] “Mete.” 382a3}}; ἰδίως, opposed to κοινῶς, λέγεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.41}} F. (but in Grammars, to be used **as a proper name**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.13)}}; **in a peculiar sense** or **usage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 115; ἰ.Αἰσχύλος τὸν Ἀγαμέμνονα ἐπὶ σκηνῆς ἀναιρεῖσθαι ποιεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” argument, cf. Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1116}}; also,= **extra versum**, τὸ φεῦ ἰδίως [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 41 (variant{ἰδίᾳ}).}}]
**VI.2** **ἰδίᾳ**, _Ionic dialect_ -ιη, as _adverb_, **by oneself, privately, on one's own account**, θύοντι ἰδίῃ μούνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132, compare 192, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 467; οὔτε ἰδίᾳ οὔτε ἐν κοινῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; καὶ ἰ. καὶ δημοσίᾳ [prev. author] 3.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30b; καὶ ἰ. καὶ κοινῇ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 40.3; ἰδίᾳ ἕκαστος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946d, etc.: with _genitive_, ἰ. τῆς φρενός}} **apart from.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 102.}}
**VI.2.b** **in ordinary talk**, opposed to ὑπὸ ποιητῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363e, compare 606c; see above 111.}}
**VI.3** κατ᾽ ἰδίαν **in private**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 169; κατ᾽ ἰδίαν εἰπεῖν τινι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.21}}; κατ᾽ ἰ. λαβεῖν τινα to take him **aside**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.84.8}}; also, **separately, apart**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.120d; οἱ κατ᾽ ἰ. βίοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.71.1. (ϝίδιος “Tab.Heracl.” 1.13, +others, “Schwyzer” 324.4 (Delph., 4th c.BC), “IG” 9(1).333.12 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}, etc.; with spiritus asper, ἐκ τοῦ ηιδίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Jahresh.” 14 “Beibl.” 141 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; καθ᾽ ἱδίαν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“IG” 22.891.6, 5(1).6 (_Laconian dialect_), 9(2).66 (from Lamia), 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 3.159, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἱδδίαν probably in {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).461.26 (Thess.).)}}

2399:
** ἰδῐώτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(ἴδιος) **private person, individual**, opposed to the State, ξυμφέροντα καὶ πόλεσι καὶ ἰδιώταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, compare 3.10, “SIG” 37.3 (Teos, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 185b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.18, etc.}}; opposed to γένος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1013.6 (Chios, 4th c.BC); opposed to φατρία, [prev. work]987.28 ([prev. passage], 4th c.BC).}}
**II** **one in a private station**, opposed to to one holding public office, or taking part in public affairs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 123, +others, cf. Decrees cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.84, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.2, etc.}}; opposed to βασιλεύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.3}}; opposed to ἄρχων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 5.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 259b, “SIG” 305.71 (4th c.BC)}}; opposed to δικαστής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.24}}; opposed to πολιτευόμενος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.70}}; opposed to ῥήτωρ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 27}}; **private soldier**, opposed to στρατηγός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.11, compare “PHib.” 1.30.21 (3rd c.BC)}}; **layman**, opposed to priest, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.52 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), “PGnom.” 200 (2nd c.AD), NT.1Cor.14.16}}: as _adjective_, ἰ. ἄνδρες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 70, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.115}}; ἰ. θεοί **homely** (with play on ἴδιος), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 891.}}
**II.2** **common man, plebeian**, οἱ ἰ. καὶ πένητες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 24}}; ἰ. καὶ εὐτελής, opposed to βασιλεύς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.10.2.}}
**II.3** as _adjective_, ἰ. βίος **private** station, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 578c}}; ἰ. λόγος **everyday** speech, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 2, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 31.2.}}
**III** **one who has no professional knowledge, layman**, καὶ ἰατρὸς καὶ ἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 178c, “Lg.” 933d; ἰ. ἤ τινα τέχνην ἔχων [prev. author] “Sph.” 221c}}; of prose-writers, ἐν μέτρῳ ὡς ποιητής, ἢ ἄνευ μέτρου ὡς ἰ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 258d, compare “Smp.” 178b; ἰ. καὶ μηδὲν αὐλήσεως ἐπαΐων [prev. author] “Prt.” 327c}}; opposed to to a professed orator, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.11}}; to a trained soldier, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.1}}; ἰδιώτας, ὡς εἰπεῖν, χειροτέχναις (-νας codices) ἀνταγωνισαμένους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72}}; opposed to ἀσκητής, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.7, compare 12.1}}; opposed to ἀθλητής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116b13}}; opposed to a professed philosopher, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1266a31, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.51 O., “D.” 1.25; in Music, [prev. author] “Mus.” p.42}} K.; opposed to δημιουργός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312a, “Thg.” 124c: as}} _adjective_, ὁ ἰ. ὄχλος, opposed to artificers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 12.}}
**III.2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **unpractised, unskilled in** a thing, ἰατρικῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 345a, compare “Ti.” 20a; ἔργου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.9}}; ἰ. κατὰ τοὺς πόνους, κατὰ τὸν ὕπνον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.5.11; ἰ. τὰ ἄλλα 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.12.1; ἰ. ὡς πρὸς ἡμᾶς ἀγωνίζεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” [same place], compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 81.}}
**III.3** generally, **raw hand, ignoramus**, ἄν τε δεινοὶ λάχωσιν ἄν τε ἰδιῶται.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.35}}; παιδάρια καὶ ἰ., of slaves, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.234 (compare ἰ. οἰκέται 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 30)}}; ἀμαθὴς καὶ ἰ., opposed to τεχνίτης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ind.” 29}}; _vocative_ ἰδιῶτα, as a term of abuse, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 71.}}
**III.4** '**average man**', opposed to a person of distinction, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1104a.}}
**IV** ἰδιῶται, οἱ, **one's own countrymen**, opposed to ξένοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 459.}}
